"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][3]={"university_computer_science_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – A database on a server<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What this section is about<\/h2>\n<p>In the introductory chapter M1-L5, we learnt about tables, keys, the\ndatabase language <strong>SQL<\/strong> (<em>Structured Query\nLanguage<\/em>) and transactions. M2-L1 to M2-L5 then covered servers,\napplications and their distribution. Now we bring the two topics\ntogether: <strong>What happens inside a database server when many users\nwant to read or change data simultaneously?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A database is not simply a large file with a search function. A\nmodern <strong>database management system (DBMS)<\/strong> manages data\nformats and relationships, selects execution strategies, coordinates\nconcurrent access, protects changes against certain failures and\nenforces permissions. It must also turn thousands of individual requests\ninto an overall system that is as stable as possible.<\/p>\n<p>For now, we <strong>deliberately restrict ourselves to a single\ndatabase node<\/strong>. A node here means an independently operated\ndatabase instance with its associated resources; the node can be a\nphysical computer, a virtual machine or a managed single server. It does\nnot have to be the web server as well. Only in M3-L2–M3-L6 do we\ndistribute data and tasks across multiple database nodes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: From a local table to an online\nservice<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a small online service for managing analysis jobs. Each user\nhas an account, uploads data and retrieves the results later. For an\nindividual developer, this would initially be quite manageable: one\napplication and one database. With two visitors, the load is low.\nHowever, when many users are active simultaneously, several difficulties\ncan arise:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Many requests want to read the same data.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two operations attempt to modify the same record.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Some queries require substantial processing time or read enormous\nsections of tables.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The database server has limited processor time, <strong>RAM<\/strong>\n(<em>Random Access Memory<\/em>, main memory), space on an\n<strong>SSD<\/strong> (<em>Solid State Drive<\/em>) and network\nbandwidth.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If a power failure or crash occurs, the system needs to lose as\nlittle previously acknowledged work as possible.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The obvious question, “Do we need a distributed database\nimmediately?”, is usually premature. First, we need to understand what a\nsingle database system can already achieve and which bottlenecks\nactually occur.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-a-web-service-and-a-database\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture: A\nweb service and a database<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browsers of many users\n          │\n          ▼\n      Web application\n          │  SQL queries\n          ▼\n┌────────────────────────────┐\n│ A database server          │\n│                            │\n│ Access control             │\n│ Query planner / execution  │\n│ Transactions / locks       │\n│ Buffer cache               │\n│ Logging                    │\n│                            │\n│ Tables, indexes, log file  │\n└─────────────┬──────────────┘\n              │\n          SSD / storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The blocks represent functions and layers, not necessarily\nindependent programs. A database instance can provide all these\nfunctions in a single server installation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What a table means internally<\/h2>\n<p>From the application’s perspective, a relational table looks like an\nordered list of columns containing many rows. However, the rows have\n<strong>no generally guaranteed physical order<\/strong>. Without an\nexplicit sorting instruction, the application must not rely on any\nparticular order in which query results appear.<\/p>\n<p>A schematic table <code>auftraege<\/code> could look like this:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">auftrag_id<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">kunde_id<\/th>\n<th>status<\/th>\n<th>erstellt_am<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">101<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>wartend<\/td>\n<td>10:00<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">102<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>fertig<\/td>\n<td>10:03<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">103<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td>verarbeitet<\/td>\n<td>10:05<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Record<\/strong> and <strong>row<\/strong> refer to the same\nlogical entry here. The <code>auftrag_id<\/code> can be the\n<strong>primary key<\/strong>: a unique identifier. The\n<code>kunde_id<\/code> can refer to the customer table. Such\nrelationships and integrity rules help to prevent inconsistencies.<\/p>\n<p>Physically, the database usually stores the data in <strong>pages or\nblocks<\/strong> – small, manageable units of storage. This is an\nimportant distinction from the idea that each record might be a separate\nfile. A write operation can affect several rows on the same page, and\nthe system must decide which pages to keep in main memory.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem this organisation solves:<\/strong> A DBMS can manage\ndata, free space and changes more efficiently instead of leaving each\napplication to invent its own file formats. <strong>When is it\nunnecessary?<\/strong> For a single immutable configuration file, a fully\nfledged server database is usually excessive.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How an individual read query is processed<\/h2>\n<p>A typical request in ordinary language is: “Show the last twenty jobs\nfor this customer.” The application submits an SQL query. The following\nthen happens, in simplified form:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_016_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simplified path of an SQL read query: SQL and permissions, selection of the access plan, table scan or index access, execution and result.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: After the SQL query and permission checks, the\noptimiser chooses a table scan or index access path before filtering the\nresult.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>One possible path from SQL to the result set; real systems use\ndifferent optimisations.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The database checks whether the query is syntactically valid and\nwhether the accessing role has the necessary permissions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It identifies the tables and columns accessed by the query.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The <strong>query planner<\/strong> (or optimiser) assesses possible\nstrategies: read the whole table, use an index or join tables in a\ndifferent order.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An <strong>execution plan<\/strong> defines an appropriate sequence\nof operations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The executor reads the necessary data from main memory or the SSD if\nrequired, filters and sorts it and returns the result.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>With prepared queries, parts of this processing can be reused; a real\ndatabase does not necessarily repeat every step from scratch for each\nquery.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application: “20 latest jobs for customer 7”\n                     │\n                     ▼\n                  Parse SQL\n                     │\n                     ▼\n              Check permissions\n                     │\n                     ▼\n          Select suitable access path\n              │              │\n          Table scan      Index access\n              └──────┬───────┘\n                     ▼\n             Load / filter data\n                     │\n                     ▼\n               Sort / limit\n                     │\n                     ▼\n               20 result rows<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This already provides a major opportunity for improving performance:\nwhat matters is not merely the total amount of stored data, but\n<strong>how many data blocks actually need to be read and processed for\na particular query<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Table scans and indexes: Why a million rows are not\nautomatically slow<\/h2>\n<p>Without a suitable search aid, the database can inspect a table from\nbeginning to end. This is called a <strong>sequential scan<\/strong>.\nWith a large table and a rarely occurring customer number, this can be\ncostly.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_017_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Simplified B+ tree index: For a search for ID 50, the root with separator value 40 leads to the right leaf containing 40, 50 and 60 and then to the record.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Searching for key 50 in the simplified B+ tree\nfollows the root separator 40 to the right-hand leaf containing the\nkey.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A B+ tree can locate a requested key ID via the tree levels rather\nthan scanning all rows in sequence.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>index<\/strong> is an additional data structure that\nassociates search values with locations or other information important\nfor the search. A common index type is the <strong>B-tree<\/strong>: a\nbalanced search tree suited to many equality, range and sorting\nqueries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Illustrative comparison:<\/strong> You are looking for\n“transaction” in a textbook. Without an index, you might have to turn\nthe pages of the entire book. With an index, you can locate relevant\npages much faster. However, the index takes up extra space and must be\nmaintained when the book changes.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Table with many jobs\n┌──────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 101, 102, 103, 104, 105, ...     │\n└──────────────────────────────────┘\n          │\n     Index on kunde_id\n          │\n          ▼\n     Customer 7 → matching entries\n          │\n          ▼\n     Read only the required rows<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An index is <strong>not a magical way to speed up every\nquery<\/strong>. If almost every row is returned, a sequential scan may\nbe cheaper. An index requires storage, increases backup sizes and can\nslow down changes because the index structure must also be updated. An\nindex on a field that takes very few different values is not\nautomatically advantageous. The benefit depends on query patterns and\nthe data set.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"composite-indexes\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Composite indexes<\/h3>\n<p>For a typical query, “Jobs for one customer, newest first”, a\n<strong>composite index<\/strong> on <code>(kunde_id, erstellt_am)<\/code>\nmay be useful. The data is then already organised favourably for certain\nsearch and sorting conditions. Which parts of a multicolumn index can be\nused in a specific query depends on the database system, operators and\nsort order.<\/p>\n<p>For small data sets, such optimisation is often unnecessary.\n<strong>A handful of useful indexes is better than reflexively indexing\nevery column.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The query planner decides – not the programmer alone<\/h2>\n<p>Even if an index exists, the database does not have to use it. The\nquery planner assesses the estimated costs of different execution\nstrategies, drawing on <strong>statistics<\/strong> about the number and\ndistribution of values in a table, among other things.<\/p>\n<p>For example, if almost all jobs have the status <code>fertig<\/code>,\nan index on that column might be of little use for the request “Show all\ncompleted jobs”. However, if the application searches for a very rare\nerror status, the same index can be useful. <strong>Selectivity<\/strong>\ndescribes how strongly a condition restricts the number of candidate\nrows.<\/p>\n<p>PostgreSQL provides a tool for this, <code>EXPLAIN<\/code>: it shows\nthe planned execution strategy, such as a table scan or an index access.\n<code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE<\/code> actually executes the query and measures\nits execution. With SQL write statements, this can modify data; it is\ntherefore not a harmless inspection-only operation.<\/p>\n<p>The most important principle is: <strong>Understand a slow query\nbefore optimising it.<\/strong> There is no reason to reflexively\npurchase additional servers as long as an unsuitable search condition or\na missing index is the main cause.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html\">PostgreSQL:\nUsing EXPLAIN<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-examine.html\">Examining\nIndex Usage<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>, checked on 03.11.2026.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Caching: Data need not always come from the SSD<\/h2>\n<p>Databases often have their own <strong>buffer pools<\/strong> or page\ncaches. These hold data blocks that are currently needed in main memory.\nThe operating system can also use a file cache. PostgreSQL uses the\n<code>shared_buffers<\/code> parameter for the shared database buffer,\namong other settings. The precise allocation is a technical\nconfiguration question; no universal percentage rule can set it\ncorrectly for every workload.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>cache hit<\/strong> means that a required memory page is\nalready quickly accessible. A <strong>cache miss<\/strong> means that it\nmust be fetched from a slower storage layer. The term “cache” here\ntherefore does not simply refer to the web cache from M2-L5. Caches\nexist at different levels.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Database requests a data block\n              │\n              ▼\n       Database buffer (RAM)\n       │             │\n      Hit           Miss\n       │             │\n       │             ▼\n       │        File cache / SSD\n       │             │\n       └──────┬──────┘\n              ▼\n        Process records<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What does this improve?<\/strong> Repeated accesses to the\nsame data can become significantly faster. <strong>When does it help\nlittle?<\/strong> If every query reads entirely different, very large\nregions of data, the important data may no longer fit in memory. The I/O\nload (<em>Input/Output<\/em>, reading and writing data) is then\ncrucial.<\/p>\n<p>More RAM is therefore sometimes a good investment, but it does not\nreplace sensible queries. Conversely, a poorly designed query can\nconsume considerable CPU time and perform unnecessary sorting even with\nplenty of main memory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens when writing data<\/h2>\n<p>A read query does not initially change anything. A write operation –\nsuch as “Set job 101 to completed” – has considerably more to\nconsider:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the change permitted, and does it satisfy the data types and\nintegrity rules?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is another operation currently working on the same record?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which table and index pages will change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When is the change considered acknowledged?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can a consistent state be restored after a crash?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A widespread technique is called <strong>Write-Ahead Logging\n(WAL)<\/strong>, literally logging in advance. PostgreSQL records changes\nin a designated log structure before the corresponding modified data\npages have to be written persistently. This allows the system to replay\nchanges from the log after certain crashes. The precise guarantees\ndepend on factors including the database configuration and reliability\nof the storage hardware.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application changes job status\n               │\n               ▼\n       Database checks change\n               │\n               ▼\n       Change information to WAL\n               │\n               ▼\n       Transaction COMMIT (confirm)\n               │\n               ▼\n       Data pages are managed as\n       appropriate and written later<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This sketch shows the underlying idea, not the complete internal\nsequence of all steps. In particular, it <strong>does not<\/strong> mean\nthat all modified table pages must already be written straight to the\nSSD when a change is acknowledged. Nor does the existence of a WAL\n<strong>mean<\/strong> that data is protected against accidental\ndeletion, ransomware, loss of hardware or corruption of the entire\nstorage environment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:<\/strong> WAL contributes to\nrecoverability and durability, but it is <strong>not an independent\nexternal backup<\/strong>. We discuss backups and testing their\nrecoverability in detail in M3-L6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-intro.html\">PostgreSQL:\nWrite-Ahead Logging<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>, checked on 03.11.2026.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A transaction is more than an individual SQL statement<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>transaction<\/strong> groups related database operations\ninto a logical unit. Example: A paid analysis package is credited to an\naccount, and a corresponding accounting entry is stored at the same\ntime. It would be problematic if only one of the two changes took\npermanent effect.<\/p>\n<p>The well-known acronym <strong>ACID<\/strong> summarises important\nproperties:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter<\/th>\n<th>Full form<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in the example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td>Atomicity<\/td>\n<td>Either all related changes are applied or none are<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td>Consistency<\/td>\n<td>Defined integrity rules are preserved<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I<\/td>\n<td>Isolation<\/td>\n<td>Concurrent operations are isolated from each other according to the\nchosen isolation level<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<td>Durability<\/td>\n<td>Acknowledged changes should survive a crash according to the\npromised durability conditions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This does not mean that the database automatically knows every\nbusiness rule. If the application performs an incorrect calculation, “C”\ndoes not ensure a correct business model by itself. Business rules must\nbe explicitly modelled and enforced.<\/p>\n<p>A second widespread error is keeping transactions open for too long.\nWhile a transaction is active, locks can remain in place, storage or\nversion cleanup may be hindered, or other operations may have to wait.\n<strong>Short, clearly bounded transactions<\/strong> are therefore often\nan important operational characteristic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens when many readers are active\nsimultaneously?<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose 1,000 people are logged into a website. This does\n<strong>not<\/strong> mean that 1,000 database queries are executed\nsimultaneously. Many users are reading a page, thinking or inactive.\nWhat matters is how many requests arrive per unit of time, how long they\ntake and which resources they require.<\/p>\n<p>If many queries read the same frequently needed information, indexes\nand caches can save substantial work. However, if large tables are\nqueried without suitable filters, the same data may have to be processed\nagain and again. Utilisation then increases.<\/p>\n<p>The database server also needs connections for concurrent access. A\n<strong>connection<\/strong> is a logical session or communication link\nto the DBMS. For systems with relatively high costs per connection, a\n<strong>connection pool<\/strong> can help: the application maintains a\nlimited number of reusable database connections and distributes queries\nacross them.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Many browsers\n      │\n      ▼\nWeb application with many calls\n      │\n      ▼\nConnection pool (limited number of connections)\n   │      │      │\n   └──────┼──────┘\n          ▼\n      Database server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A pool does not provide unlimited acceleration. If all queries are\ncompeting for a small number of connections, queues develop. However, it\noften solves the problem of having to establish and close thousands of\nshort-lived connections at considerable cost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrent reading and writing: Versions instead of blanket\nblocking<\/h2>\n<p>With several users, it becomes particularly important <strong>which\nversion of the data a reader sees while another user writes<\/strong>.\nPostgreSQL uses <strong>MVCC<\/strong> (<em>Multi-Version Concurrency\nControl<\/em>), among other techniques. In simplified terms, different\ntransactions can see different versions of a record depending on when\ntheir consistent view of the data was determined.<\/p>\n<p>Example:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">User A reads the status of a job: <code>wartend<\/code>\n(waiting).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">User B sets the same job to <code>fertig<\/code> (completed) and\ncommits the change.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Depending on the isolation level and query time, user A may still\nsee the old status or see the new status in a new query.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This seems strange at first, but it makes sense. It prevents every\nordinary read query from having to block all write operations\nindiscriminately. Database rules determine which states an operation is\nallowed to observe.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Time ─────────────────────────────────────►\n\nTransaction A: reads version “waiting”\nTransaction B:              changes → “completed” → COMMIT\nTransaction A: depending on isolation, still the old snapshot\n               or a later query with a new view<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The limitation is important: <strong>MVCC does not eliminate all\nlocks.<\/strong> Two writes to the same record can still compete. For\nexample, an account balance must not silently become incorrect because\nof two uncoordinated debits. Concurrent transactions can wait, fail\nbecause of each other or – with an unfavourable lock order – enter a\n<strong>deadlock<\/strong> (mutual blocking). Databases have mechanisms\nfor handling such conflicts, but the application must deal correctly\nwith errors and retry attempts.<\/p>\n<p>Maintaining old data versions also involves work. PostgreSQL uses\n<strong>VACUUM<\/strong>, among other mechanisms, to clean up row\nversions or storage areas that are no longer needed. We examine the\ndetails in M3-L5. Already at this point, it is important to note that\nmany long-running transactions can impair database health.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html\">PostgreSQL:\nMVCC Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>, checked on 03.11.2026.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When many users write concurrently<\/h2>\n<p>For read queries, an essential question is: how much must be read?\nFor writes, there is an additional question: <strong>Do several\ntransactions want to modify the same resources?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>An example of less problematic concurrency: several customers\nindependently create new analysis jobs. The database can process many\noperations simultaneously insofar as its architecture and resources\nallow.<\/p>\n<p>An example of conflicts: thousands of jobs constantly update the same\nglobal statistics row. This creates a <strong>hotspot<\/strong> – a\nheavily contested record. Additional processors or more web servers do\nnot automatically solve this problem. A different data model often\nhelps: log individual events separately and aggregate the statistics\nlater, provided business requirements allow it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1000 independent writes\n            │\n            ▼\n       Different records\n            │\n     often easily parallelised\n\n1000 writes to the same row\n            │\n            ▼\n       Conflicts / waiting<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The figures are illustrative. Actual performance depends on factors\nincluding transaction duration, indexes, storage system, database\nimplementation and hardware. <strong>More concurrent work can initially\nimprove throughput but may significantly worsen response times beyond a\ncertain point.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Understanding performance: Latency, throughput and\nqueues<\/h2>\n<p>Two performance concepts should be distinguished:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Latency:<\/strong> How long does it take for a particular\nrequest to be answered?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Throughput:<\/strong> How many queries or jobs are processed\nin a given time?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A database can answer individual requests very quickly and still be\noverwhelmed by extreme concurrent load. Conversely, a data-intensive\nanalysis can take a long time while a server successfully processes many\nsuch analyses over an hour.<\/p>\n<p>When requests arrive faster than the system can complete them, a\n<strong>queue<\/strong> develops. Its length grows, response times rise\nand eventually connections or time limits may be exhausted. This is not\nan error in the database logic, but a consequence of limited\ncapacity.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"simplified-load-example\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simplified load example<\/h3>\n<p>In a <em>purely hypothetical<\/em> example, a small database server\ncan continuously process 100 similar queries per second. If only 20\narrive per second, spare capacity remains. If 120 arrive per second\ncontinuously, a backlog grows unless the load is limited or reduced. The\nfact that a query takes 10 milliseconds while the server is idle does\nnot mean that a single server can continuously finish 100 concurrent\nrequests in the same amount of time under arbitrary load.<\/p>\n<p>These illustrative figures are <strong>not benchmarks<\/strong> for\nPostgreSQL, SQLite or any cloud provider. They merely show why capacity\nand waiting times must be considered together.<\/p>\n<p>The main conclusion: a larger number of users does not necessarily\nrequire a second database server. It often helps first to limit\nrequests, use caches sensibly, avoid unnecessary database calls or move\ntime-consuming work into background jobs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The five typical resource bottlenecks<\/h2>\n<p>A single database server has several limited resources. These can\nproduce quite different symptoms.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bottleneck<\/th>\n<th>Typical cause<\/th>\n<th>Possible initial approach<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CPU<\/strong> (<em>Central Processing Unit<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Many costly queries, sorts and calculations<\/td>\n<td>Inspect query plans, avoid unnecessary computation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RAM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Working data exceeds buffer capacity, or sort/hash operations need\nsubstantial memory<\/td>\n<td>Examine memory demand and workload, adjust RAM<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SSD / I/O<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Reading, writing or synchronising many data blocks with durability\nguarantees<\/td>\n<td>Examine indexes, cache, storage latency and write patterns<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Network<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Large results or high communication overhead between application and\nDBMS<\/td>\n<td>Transfer less data, combine queries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locks / conflicts<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Many transactions wait for the same rows or resources<\/td>\n<td>Shorten transactions, examine hotspots and the data model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A common misconception is: “CPU utilisation is only 15 per cent, so\neverything must be fast.” In fact, the server might spend most of its\ntime waiting for the SSD or another transaction. Similarly, there may be\nsubstantial free RAM while the application still sends too much\nunnecessary data over the network.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The measured cause of the bottleneck, not the largest server\nconfiguration, determines the appropriate action.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How to identify a bottleneck without optimising\nblindly<\/h2>\n<p>Database systems provide diagnostic facilities. PostgreSQL, for\nexample, supplies statistics about active sessions, table accesses and\nvarious I/O operations. The additional module\n<code>pg_stat_statements<\/code> can collect aggregate metrics for\nfrequently executed SQL statements if it is configured\nappropriately.<\/p>\n<p>Five questions provide a useful framework for analysis:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Which query is slow?<\/strong> One specific query or every\nquery?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What kind of load occurs?<\/strong> Many readers, many\nwriters or a few very large analyses?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What is the system waiting for?<\/strong> Processor, storage,\nnetwork, lock or free connection?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Which execution plan does it use?<\/strong> Is a table scan\nappropriate or avoidable?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What has changed?<\/strong> More data, a different index, a\nnew software version, more users or a new background job?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Suitable measures can then be compared systematically. These include\nrevising an index, changing an SQL query, adding a cache, using a more\npowerful single server or pooling database connections. Only when the\nbottleneck still cannot be resolved in a reasonable manner does a split\nacross multiple database nodes become a serious option.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html\">PostgreSQL:\nMonitoring Statistics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html\"><code>pg_stat_statements<\/code><span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\nchecked on 03.11.2026.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SQLite versus server databases: What changes in the\narchitecture?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>SQLite<\/strong> is an embedded database system. The\napplication uses a library that accesses a local database file;\nnormally, no separately accessible database server process is running.\nThis makes SQLite especially attractive for desktop software, local\ntools, catalogues and small, controlled applications.<\/p>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong> is a client-server system. Applications\ncommunicate with an independent database server that centrally manages\nconnections, permissions, transactions and competing requests. This is\nespecially helpful when many different application processes need to\naccess shared data simultaneously.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local application with SQLite:\nDesktop program → SQLite library → Database file\n\nOnline application with PostgreSQL:\nBrowser → Web backend → PostgreSQL process → Storage device<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>SQLite also supports transactions and multiple concurrent readers. In\n<strong>WAL mode<\/strong> (<em>Write-Ahead Logging<\/em>), readers and\none writer can be active at the same time. However, <strong>only one\nwrite operation at a time<\/strong> is possible, which can become a\nbottleneck for write-intensive applications. This distinction should not\nbe confused with the claim that “SQLite cannot support multiple users at\nall”: that would be too broad and incorrect. What matters is how\napplications access the database and what concurrency is required. WAL\nmode and access via network file systems require particular caution;\nSQLite’s usual WAL mode is not intended for processes on different hosts\nusing a shared network file system.<\/p>\n<p>PostgreSQL permits multiple concurrent write transactions to the\nextent that they do not conflict with each other and sufficient\nresources are available. This does not mean that arbitrary levels of\nconcurrency come at no cost.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\">SQLite: Write-Ahead Logging<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\nchecked on 03.11.2026.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"decision-aid\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision aid<\/h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Often an appropriate starting point<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A local PureBasic media catalogue<\/td>\n<td>SQLite<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A small local utility with settings and lists<\/td>\n<td>SQLite or a simple file<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>An online service with many separate backend processes and user\naccounts<\/td>\n<td>A relational database server such as PostgreSQL<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A small publicly accessible service on one host<\/td>\n<td>PostgreSQL or, depending on write load and operational model,\nSQLite<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A large organisation with very many transactions<\/td>\n<td>Initially a carefully sized server DBMS; distribution only after\nrequirements analysis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These are architectural heuristics, not immutable product limits.\nRequirements concerning data protection, availability, data volume and\nbackup processes can change the decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The invisible work: Operations, security and costs<\/h2>\n<p>A database server requires more than running DBMS software. Reliable\noperation involves at least the following:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Access permissions:<\/strong> Application processes receive\nonly the permissions they need; administrator and application accounts\nremain separate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Network boundaries:<\/strong> An internal database is\ngenerally not made directly accessible from the public internet without\ngood reason.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Safe inputs:<\/strong> Parameterised queries help prevent SQL\ninjection – the introduction of foreign SQL commands via uncontrolled\ninputs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Updates:<\/strong> Security updates, scheduled maintenance\nwindows where necessary and application compatibility with new\nversions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Backups:<\/strong> Verified backup copies with appropriate\nretention and restoration tests.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Monitoring:<\/strong> Observing capacity, runtimes, errors,\nfree storage space and unusual access patterns.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Data protection:<\/strong> Consideration of personal data,\nretention periods, deletion and access permissions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Even with a <strong>managed database service<\/strong>, the provider\ndoes not automatically take over all these tasks. The provider may, for\nexample, manage hardware, the operating system and certain database\nupdates; however, business data models, roles, secure application\nqueries and backup suitability remain at least partly the operator’s\nresponsibility. The exact scope depends on the contract and product.<\/p>\n<p>Costs arise from server resources, SSD storage, I/O load, data\nbackups, data transfer, monitoring and, above all, operating time or\nadministrative effort. In many cases, a single appropriately sized\ndatabase server is easier to operate than a database cluster distributed\nprematurely. Specific prices depend on the provider and the time; this\nchapter therefore uses no invented prices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three examples: From a media catalogue to AI\nprocessing<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A – Local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p>You manage an extensive collection of metadata, file paths and\nkeywords. An SQLite file with suitable indexes can be perfectly adequate\nfor this. If searching slows down, the cause may be missing indexes or\nunsuitable queries, rather than “too little cloud”. A public server\ndatabase could add complexity without a real benefit.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-paid-online-analysis-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B – Paid online\nanalysis service<\/h3>\n<p>Many customers sign in, submit analysis jobs and query their status.\nA web backend with a central PostgreSQL server can manage these data\nconsistently. Initially, the priorities are suitable indexes on\n<code>kunde_id<\/code> and job status, short transactions, sensible\nconnection pools and reliable backups. As usage grows, measurements\nestablish whether the database, rather than an external computation\nprogram, really is the bottleneck.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-ai-job-platform\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C – AI job platform<\/h3>\n<p>A web service accepts requests. A database manages job statuses,\naccess permissions and references to results. A <strong>queue<\/strong>\nand several <strong>workers<\/strong> (job processors) can take over\ntime-consuming image or text generation. In this model the database\nserver is primarily the <strong>coordination and metadata\ninstance<\/strong> – not automatically the place where large models\nperform computations or image files must be permanently stored.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User → API → Database: create job\n             │\n             └→ Queue → AI worker → Result store\n                          │\n                          └→ Database: update status<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If 20 AI workers finish simultaneously, the database needs to process\n20 status changes. This usually creates a different load profile from 20\nenormous model computations. With this separation, one database server\ncan remain sufficient for a long time while computational processing\ngrows independently. We examine how reliable queues and retries work in\nM4-L1 and M3-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When should we even consider multiple database\nservers?<\/h2>\n<p>A single database instance has limits: it can fail, storage resources\nare finite and all changes must be coordinated according to the rules of\nthat system. If a service has very high availability requirements,\nserves many geographically distant users or has a data volume that no\nlonger fits economically on one node, additional database nodes can be\nuseful.<\/p>\n<p>However, <strong>more nodes do not automatically create more usable\nwrite capacity.<\/strong> They also raise new questions: which changes\nare copied where? What happens during network outages? Which node is\nauthorised to write? When does a reader see a new version? How is a\nvalid operational state restored after a failure?<\/p>\n<p>We have thus arrived precisely at the boundary between an individual\ndatabase server and a distributed system.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"transition-to-the-next-lesson\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transition to the next\nlesson<\/h3>\n<p>In <strong>M3-L2 – Replication and high availability<\/strong>, we\nexamine how data is maintained on additional servers, when a replica can\nhelp and why replication is <strong>not a substitute for\nbackups<\/strong>. We then cover sharding in M3-L3 – the actual\npartitioning of data across separate nodes – and different architectural\napproaches to distributed databases in M3-L4.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A database is an active system comprising data, indexes, query\nplanning, memory buffers, transactions and access control.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Many rows are not automatically a problem. Poor queries, missing\nindexes, unnecessary data transfer and hotspots can slow down even\nsmaller data sets.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Concurrent reading and writing requires explicit rules for\nvisibility and conflict management. MVCC helps but does not eliminate\nall locks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">WAL assists recovery after certain failures but <strong>does not\nreplace a backup<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More servers are one possible later solution – usually\n<strong>not<\/strong> the first measure to take when a database server\nseems slow.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Review date: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> Primary sources, using the\ncurrent vendor or project documentation as the starting point in each\ncase:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-intro.html\">Introduction\nto Indexes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – the purpose and maintenance of indexes and the\nselection of access paths.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html\">Using\nEXPLAIN<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – query planning, plans and measured execution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html\">Multiversion\nConcurrency Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – visibility of data versions and\nconcurrency.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-intro.html\">Write-Ahead\nLogging<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – logging and recovery.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html\">Resource\nConsumption<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – memory and I/O configuration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html\">Monitoring\nStatistics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html\"><code>pg_stat_statements<\/code><span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– diagnostics and query statistics.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite: <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\">Write-Ahead\nLogging<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – reader/writer concurrency and limits of WAL mode.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> The teaching examples and architectural\ndecisions in this chapter are simplified educational models. They aim to\nmake operating principles and typical trade-offs understandable, not to\nprovide immutable performance figures, legal assurances or product\nrecommendations.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – Replication and high availability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Why a second database becomes interesting in the\nfirst place<\/h2>\n<p>In M3-L1, we saw how a single database server stores and retrieves\ninformation, speeds up queries with indexes and coordinates concurrent\nchanges. This works remarkably well for many applications. But now\nimagine that the database contains the job data of a paid online\nservice. The server fails because of a hardware fault, or the operating\nsystem urgently needs an update. Can users continue working?<\/p>\n<p>One possible answer is to keep a <strong>second database server\nready<\/strong> that follows the data and changes of the first. This is\ncalled <strong>replication<\/strong>. It can solve several problems:\nspeeding up recovery after a failure, shifting read queries to other\ncomputers or making data available at other locations.<\/p>\n<p>The crucial qualification is that <strong>a second copy of the data\nis not yet a complete high-availability system.<\/strong> The servers\nmust receive changes, detect a failure, coordinate the transition to a\nreplacement, redirect application traffic and prevent two machines from\naccepting conflicting changes without control. A replica is not a\nsubstitute for independent backups either.<\/p>\n<p>This lesson covers the progression from a simple copy to a working,\nmonitored failover strategy. We use PostgreSQL as a concrete,\nwell-documented example; the fundamental problems also apply to other\ndatabase systems. Individual terms and guarantees differ by product and\nconfiguration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The most important terms – clearly distinguished<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Replication<\/strong> means transferring data or changes from\none system to another to establish a corresponding dataset there. It is\na <em>method of transferring and synchronising data<\/em>, not in itself\na guarantee of accessibility.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>primary server<\/strong> (<em>primary<\/em>) is the\ndatabase node that accepts changes in our basic model. A\n<strong>replica<\/strong> or <strong>standby<\/strong> is another node\nthat follows the primary server’s data state. Not every type of\nreplication is limited to this arrangement, but it is the easiest model\nto start with.<\/p>\n<p><strong>High availability<\/strong> (HA) describes the goal of\nproviding a service despite certain failures, with interruptions as\nshort as possible. It may involve redundant computers, resilient\nnetworks, monitoring, controlled switching and tested recovery\nprocedures. High availability does <em>not<\/em> mean absolute continuity\nwithout interruption.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Failover<\/strong> is the transition from a failed primary\nserver to a replacement. A <strong>switchover<\/strong>, by contrast, is\na planned, controlled role change, for example before maintenance. A\n<strong>read replica<\/strong> is a replica that serves read requests; a\nstandby instance does not necessarily have to accept user queries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Recovery<\/strong> means restoring operation after a fault. It\nmay involve restarting an individual process, having a standby take over\nor restoring a backup. These methods differ in duration and in their\nability to restore earlier data states.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-basic-concept\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture: Basic\nconcept<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                     Online application\n                            │\n                            ▼\n                      Primary database\n                      (reads + writes)\n                            │\n                            │ transfer changes\n                            ▼\n                         Replica\n                    (replay data changes)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The two boxes may represent different physical servers, virtual\nmachines or managed database instances. If they were merely two\nprocesses on the same faulty disk, there would be little protection from\na storage device failure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is actually copied?<\/h2>\n<p>One might think that the primary server sends the entire database\nafter each change. That would be inefficient for large datasets.\nInstead, many systems start with an initial dataset and subsequently\ntransfer only further changes.<\/p>\n<p>PostgreSQL supports <strong>physical replication<\/strong>, among\nother methods. A standby follows changes using the <strong>Write-Ahead\nLog (WAL)<\/strong>, a log of changes written in advance, which we\nencountered in M3-L1. WAL records can be sent continuously over a\nconnection (<em>streaming replication<\/em>) or made available through\narchived log files (<em>log shipping<\/em>). The standby replays the\nchanges and thus approaches the state of the primary server.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>       Primary server                   Standby\n  ┌──────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────┐\n  │ Tables + indexes │             │ Tables + indexes │\n  │ Transactions     │             │ WAL replay       │\n  └────────┬─────────┘             └────────▲─────────┘\n           │                                │\n           ▼                                │\n       WAL records ─── continuously ────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In practice, a new standby does not start with an empty database. It\nfirst needs a consistent initial dataset, often obtained through a\n<strong>base backup<\/strong>. It can then replay subsequent WAL changes.\nSuch a copy must correspond correctly to the log position; arbitrary\nfile copies made while the system is running are insufficient.<\/p>\n<p>In PostgreSQL, <strong>physical replication<\/strong> concerns the\nentire database cluster in the PostgreSQL sense: the associated server\ninstance and its data directory. It is not the same as selectively\ndistributing individual tables. Its advantage is that it follows the\nsame database instance relatively directly. Its disadvantage is that an\nindividual functional area cannot easily be handled independently;\ncompatibility between different major versions is also limited.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"logical-replication-a-different-layer\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logical replication – a\ndifferent layer<\/h3>\n<p>PostgreSQL also offers <strong>logical replication<\/strong>. It\ntransfers logical data changes to published data objects and typically\nfollows a <em>publish–subscribe<\/em> model: a publisher makes data\nchanges available, and a subscriber receives and processes them. This\nallows selected tables, for example, to be transferred selectively. It\nis useful for data integration or migrations, but <strong>is not\nautomatically a drop-in replacement for a physically replicated failover\nnode<\/strong>. Schema changes, sequence positions and conflicts may\nrequire additional handling.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Physical: Primary instance ── WAL / storage changes ──► Standby instance\nLogical:  Selected table changes ─────────────────────► Subscriber<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>We examine the choice between these variants in more detail in M3-L4.\nFor the following failure scenarios, we stay with the typical physical\nprimary/standby configuration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Asynchronous replication – fast, but with a delay<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>asynchronous replication<\/strong>, the primary server\ndoes not have to wait for acknowledgement from the replica before\nconfirming the successful completion of a transaction to the\napplication. This reduces the waiting time of a normal write\noperation.<\/p>\n<p>For example, a user saves a new order at 12:00:00. The primary server\nconfirms that it has been saved. The replica may not yet have received\nor processed the change at that moment. It catches up a little\nlater.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Time                Primary server               Replica\n12:00:00            order saved                  previous state\n12:00:00            ‘success’ to application     previous state\n12:00:01            further work                 change applied<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The one second in this example is <strong>chosen purely for\nillustration<\/strong>, not a guaranteed typical or maximum delay. In\nreal systems, the delay may be very small but can also increase\nconsiderably under load, for example because of slow networks, limited\nresources or a disrupted connection.<\/p>\n<p>This delay is called <strong>replication lag<\/strong>. It can be\nmeasured in different ways: changes have been generated but not sent;\nsent but not received; received but not durably written; durably written\nbut not yet visible to read queries. The statement ‘the replica is only\none second behind’ must therefore be defined precisely.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"what-happens-if-the-primary-server-fails-at-this-exact-moment\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What\nhappens if the primary server fails at this exact moment?<\/h3>\n<p>If the primary server has already acknowledged a change but the\nstandby has not yet received it, that change may be lost when switching\nservers. A small loss may be tolerable for an image gallery with\noccasional non-critical metadata changes. For a firmly confirmed order\nor payment transaction, it is often unacceptable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Advantage of asynchronous replication:<\/strong> low\nadditional waiting time for most writes, often practical over longer\ndistances as well. <strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> a possible data gap\nduring an unplanned transition, and potentially outdated read results on\nreplicas.<\/p>\n<p>Asynchronous replication is not ‘wrong’; it represents a deliberate\ntrade-off between response time, distance, tolerance of data loss and\navailability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synchronous replication – stronger acknowledgements, but\nmore dependencies<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>synchronous replication<\/strong>, for transactions\nconfigured accordingly, the primary server waits for feedback from one\nor more selected standbys before acknowledging completion. The goal is\nto protect the already acknowledged data state more effectively against\nthe failure of an individual machine.<\/p>\n<p>To illustrate:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>   Application        Primary server               Synchronous standby\n       │                    │                               │\n       ├─ Write request ───►│                               │\n       │                    ├── Transfer change ───────────►│\n       │                    │◄──── Agreed acknowledgement ─┤\n       │◄── ‘Success’ ──────┤                               │<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The crucial word is <strong>agreed<\/strong> acknowledgement.\nPostgreSQL has different levels. With ordinary synchronous\nacknowledgement, for example, the primary server can wait until the\ncorresponding change is durably stored in the WAL of the selected\nstandby. With <code>remote_write<\/code>, a weaker acknowledgement of the\nwrite to the standby’s operating system is sufficient; with\n<code>remote_apply<\/code>, the transaction additionally waits until the\nchange is applied on the standby and becomes visible to read queries\nthere. These levels offer different guarantees and introduce different\ndelays.<\/p>\n<p>The simplified statement ‘synchronous means that every table is\nimmediately in the same visible state on all servers’ therefore does not\nhold. What matters is <strong>which standbys participate and exactly\nwhat the acknowledgement waits for<\/strong>. Other asynchronous replicas\nmay still lag behind.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A qualification regarding the actual failover:<\/strong> even\na successfully acknowledged synchronous transaction does not prove that\n<em>every possible<\/em> standby has the acknowledged state. If a replica\nthat is not sufficiently up to date is accidentally selected for\nfailover, acknowledged changes may be missing despite the previous use\nof synchronous replication. The acknowledgement rule, choice of new\nprimary server, protected leadership role and protection against the\nreturning old primary must therefore be designed as <strong>one coherent\nprocedure<\/strong>. The exact degree of safety depends on the\nconfiguration and the assumed failure scenario. See <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html\">PostgreSQL:\nSynchronous Replication<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and the following section on split-brain and\nfencing.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-cost-of-stronger-protection\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The cost of stronger\nprotection<\/h3>\n<p>The primary server now waits for a network connection and an\nacknowledgement from a second system. This can slow down writes. If the\nrequired synchronous standby fails and no suitable replacement is\navailable, writes requiring acknowledgement may block. A decision in\nfavour of stronger data protection can then reduce the availability of\nwrite operations.<\/p>\n<p>Several potential standbys can be provided and selected by priority\nor a <strong>quorum<\/strong> (a required number of acknowledgements from\na defined group). This offers additional flexibility but replaces\nneither carefully designed failure-handling logic nor protection against\nshared failures.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> For valuable transactions\nrequiring confirmation, where a single lost write would have serious\nconsequences. <strong>When is it unnecessary or unsuitable?<\/strong> For\ninexpensive, distant additional reporting replicas, where a small update\ndelay is acceptable and the response time of the main application\nmatters more.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Read replicas: More readers, but no additional write\ncapacity<\/h2>\n<p>Often the main problem is not writes but a very large number of\nanalytical, reporting or catalogue queries. <strong>Read\nreplicas<\/strong> can then help.<\/p>\n<p>The application continues sending requests that change data to the\nprimary server. Suitable read-only queries go to one or more replicas. A\n<strong>read router<\/strong> or the application logic decides which\nconnection to use. This does not happen merely because a replica has\nbeen started.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                           Web application\n                                 │\n                     ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n                     │                       │\n                   Writes                  Reads\n                     │                       │\n                     ▼                   Read router\n                Primary server             ┌─┴─────┐\n                     │                     │       │\n                     ├── Changes ─────────►R1      R2\n                     └── Changes ─────────────────►│<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This can keep reporting queries away from the primary server. But\n<strong>write coordination initially remains with the primary\nserver<\/strong>. If almost the entire load consists of changes to the\nsame table, additional read replicas provide little benefit. Other\nconcepts are needed to distribute write data genuinely, including\nsharding (M3-L3) or specially designed distributed databases\n(M3-L4).<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-problem-of-the-record-that-was-just-saved\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The problem of\nthe record that was ‘just saved’<\/h3>\n<p>Imagine a user changing their billing address. The application\nsuccessfully saves it on the primary server. Immediately afterwards, it\nloads the account page from an asynchronous read replica. The old\naddress may still be present there.<\/p>\n<p>This looks like an application bug, even though both servers are\nworking correctly from a technical perspective.<\/p>\n<p>A simple countermeasure is to <strong>route particularly important\nreads immediately after a change to the primary server<\/strong>. Other\npossibilities include waiting and acknowledgement procedures or\nappropriate consistency mechanisms of the system in use. The technical\nterm for the expectation of reading the new version again after one’s\nown write operation is <strong>read-your-writes\nconsistency<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Here too, the seemingly simple solution ‘more servers for reading’\nrequires additional software logic. Without careful separation, it can\nintroduce new faults.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>High availability: When a replica must take over<\/h2>\n<p>So far we have mainly considered copying data. <strong>High\navailability<\/strong> requires more: a replacement server must be able\nto assume the primary role, and the application must use the new\nconnection.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_018_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"The primary server replicates to the standby; monitoring checks for failures and coordinates a possible failover.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The primary server replicates changes to a standby;\nmonitoring and controlled failover are separate operations, not the same\nas replication.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Replication and controlled failover are different processes.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose the primary server has failed. A standby is\n<strong>promoted<\/strong> to the new primary, meaning it takes on the\nwrite role. The application or a connection broker must then be directed\nto the new primary server. A further standby is subsequently set up to\nrestore redundancy after the transition.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Before the failure:\n\nApp ──► Primary A ── Changes ──► Standby B\n\nAfter the failure is detected and the takeover is controlled:\n\nApp ──────────────────────────► Primary B\n                                  │\n                                  └─► New standby C (later)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This may look simple, but it involves many individual steps:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Monitoring detects that requests to A are no longer being answered\nreliably.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The system decides whether A has actually failed or is merely\ntemporarily unreachable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The selected standby is checked for its data state and suitability\nfor taking over.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A is prevented from continuing to accept writes at the same\ntime.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">B is designated as the new primary server.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Application traffic switches to B; existing connections and\ntransactions must deal with errors or retries.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An additional standby is set up and the new topology is\nmonitored.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> PostgreSQL includes mechanisms for\nstandby operation and promotion, but complete automatic cluster\nmanagement, including failure detection, safeguards and connection\nswitching, is an additional system task. A second PostgreSQL server\nalone does not fulfil it.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"failover-versus-planned-switchover\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Failover versus planned\nswitchover<\/h3>\n<p>In a <strong>failover<\/strong>, the previous primary server has\nfailed unexpectedly or can no longer be used reliably. The transition\nmay involve interruptions and, with an asynchronous standby, data\nloss.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>switchover<\/strong>, by contrast, is prepared: manage write\ntraffic in a controlled manner, align data states, change roles and\nredirect connections. This is suitable for maintenance, for example.\nTesting is also necessary here; a planned transition is not\nautomatically risk-free.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"who-switches-the-connection\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Who switches the connection?<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>proxy<\/strong> (an intermediary for database connections)\ncan provide a stable network name and forward connections to the\ncurrently responsible primary server. Alternatively, applications can\nre-establish their connection using a shared service address or cluster\ninformation. A simple <strong>Domain Name System (DNS)<\/strong> entry is\nnot always sufficient for a rapid transition, because existing\nconnections remain open and name resolutions may be cached.<\/p>\n<p>Even with good switching procedures, the application must consider\nthat a transaction already in progress may have been aborted. It must\nnot blindly repeat failed bookings and thereby execute them twice. We\ncover idempotency and safe retry strategies in more detail in M3-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The most dangerous misconception: A failed server has not\nalways actually failed<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine two database servers in different buildings. Suddenly, the\nconnection between them breaks. A no longer responds to B’s control\nmessages. Does that mean A is broken? <strong>No.<\/strong> Perhaps A is\nstill working and only the network connection has been interrupted.<\/p>\n<p>If B now becomes the primary server while A continues accepting\nchanges, <strong>two active primary servers with diverging data<\/strong>\nmay result. This undesirable state is called\n<strong>split-brain<\/strong> (literally, a divided brain).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             Network connection lost\n\n       A                           B\n  ‘I am the primary’          ‘I am the primary’\n      │                            │\n  Customer 1 writes            Customer 2 writes\n      │                            │\n  Data state A                 Data state B\n\n      → Conflicts, possible data loss<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>To prevent this, a reliable authority must decide <strong>who has\npermission to write<\/strong>. Different cluster architectures use\nvoting, quorum rules, external decision services or the technical\nshutdown or isolation of the old node.<\/p>\n<p>One term used for this is <strong>fencing<\/strong>: the previous node\nis isolated so that it cannot continue writing to the same data. The\ndramatically named technique <strong>STONITH<\/strong> (<em>Shoot The\nOther Node In The Head<\/em>) refers to a variant in which the\nproblematic system is deliberately shut down or reset.<\/p>\n<p>A simple rule such as ‘if A does not respond for five seconds, use B’\nis therefore insufficient as a general high-availability strategy.\nNetwork partitions in particular demonstrate that <strong>failure\ndetection, decision-making and protection against conflicting\nauthority<\/strong> are three separate tasks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>High availability does not protect against every fault<\/h2>\n<p>A failure may have different causes. Not every additional copy helps\nagainst all of them.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<th>Does a replica help?<\/th>\n<th>Further action needed<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary server hardware failure<\/td>\n<td>Often yes, if in a different failure domain and failover is\ncontrolled<\/td>\n<td>Switching, monitoring, new standby<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure of the standby only<\/td>\n<td>The primary server continues working for the time being<\/td>\n<td>Repair the standby, restore redundancy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network interruption between nodes<\/td>\n<td>Not automatically<\/td>\n<td>Network diagnosis, quorum/fencing, clear leadership<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accidental deletion of all customer data<\/td>\n<td>Usually no: the deletion is replicated too<\/td>\n<td>Independent backup, recovery<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Faulty software migration<\/td>\n<td>Not reliably: a change can affect all copies<\/td>\n<td>Versioned backup, rollback/migration plan<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure of an entire data centre<\/td>\n<td>Only if the standby is genuinely independent<\/td>\n<td>Another failure domain, routing, backup strategy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compromised admin account<\/td>\n<td>Not reliably<\/td>\n<td>Limited permissions, separate backup, monitoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This illustrates a central insight: <strong>multiple copies do not\nprevent logical errors that are copied correctly.<\/strong> If an\nauthorised person deletes a table by mistake, that deletion is typically\npassed on to the replicas.<\/p>\n<p>We therefore distinguish strictly between:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Replication:<\/strong> continuously transfer data or\nchanges.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>High availability:<\/strong> reduce interruptions in\noperation for certain failures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Backup:<\/strong> be able to restore earlier, independently\nretained data states.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Disaster recovery:<\/strong> rebuild a functioning\nenvironment after more extensive damage.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Backups require a controlled retention and recovery procedure.\nReplicas are not automatically suitable for this. The interaction\nbetween backups, log archives and point-in-time recovery is covered in\nM3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two metrics: What may be lost and how long may the service\nremain unavailable?<\/h2>\n<p>Two abbreviations frequently arise in planning:<\/p>\n<p><strong>RPO<\/strong> stands for <strong>Recovery Point\nObjective<\/strong> – the target recovery point. It describes the maximum\nacceptable gap between the last backed-up or recoverable data state and\nthe failure. A low RPO means a data-loss window that is as short as\npossible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RTO<\/strong> stands for <strong>Recovery Time\nObjective<\/strong> – the target time to restore operation. It describes\nthe time within which the service should be usable again after a\ndisruption.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose a company sets the following example objective for a service:\nafter an incident, it should be operational again within an hour and\nlose no more than ten minutes’ worth of recently changed data. That is a\n<strong>planning objective<\/strong>, not a guarantee. The values must\nsuit the actual processes, risks and costs.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>        Last recoverable state                 Failure        Service usable again\n                       │                           │                    │\n  Time ────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────┼────►\n                       ◄────────── RPO ────────────►\n                                                   ◄─────── RTO ────────►<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Importantly, very low replication lag does not automatically\nguarantee a correspondingly low RPO for every type of failure. If an\nerroneous deletion has long since reached all replicas, an <em>earlier,\nindependent backup state<\/em> is decisive. Similarly, even with all the\ndata available, the RTO may be exceeded if application servers, network\nrouting or credentials do not work, for example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example A: A small online service<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine an application that analyses files and provides results to\ncustomers. Initially, only a few customers work at the same time. A\nsmall PostgreSQL instance with monitored backups may be entirely\nsufficient for this.<\/p>\n<p>As usage grows, however, occasional interruptions for maintenance\nbecome more significant. One possible extension consists of a primary\nserver and a standby. The standby follows changes. If a hardware failure\noccurs, it can take over the tasks after proper checks and\nswitching.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ─► Web app ─► Database proxy ─► Primary server\n                                            │\n                                            └──► Standby\nSeparately: tested, independently stored backup<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For such a service, the first questions to examine are: what do two\ndatabase instances cost, including storage and data traffic? Who\nmonitors replication? Who detects failures at the weekend? How is\nfailover tested? Is the application prepared for connection failures?\nHas the backup actually passed a successful test restoration?<\/p>\n<p>If a particular short recovery time is not required, <strong>a single\nwell-maintained server with reliable backups<\/strong> may be the better\nfinancial and organisational choice despite its lower availability. A\nsecond instance does not simply double every cost, but creates\nadditional resource and operational tasks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example B: Many readers, few changes<\/h2>\n<p>A large public knowledge database or media catalogue receives\nnumerous search requests but only occasional new entries. Read replicas\nmay be useful here.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             Application interface\n                   /       \\\n                Writes    Reads\n                  │          │\n               Primary     Read Replica 1\n                  │        Read Replica 2\n                  └─ Change stream ───► both<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The application must define which queries require data that is as\ncurrent as possible. Public search results often tolerate small delays.\nIn many applications, user permissions or recently saved private\nsettings require stricter handling.<\/p>\n<p>The costs are not limited to instances and storage: transfer volumes,\nnetwork connectivity, monitoring, patches, backups, fault handling and\nadditional testing effort also count. A database cluster would probably\nbe excessive for a rarely visited catalogue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example C: AI job platform<\/h2>\n<p>An AI platform processes image or text jobs using workers. The job\ndatabase stores which customer started which job, how many resources\nwere consumed and where the result is located. The actual computation\ntakes place on other computers.<\/p>\n<p>Switching on ten additional AI workers <strong>does not<\/strong> mean\nthe database automatically needs ten primary servers. Workers may use\nthe same logically central job database. Replicas could later serve\nreports and analyses of historical data, for example, while critical\nstatus transitions take place on the primary server.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User ──► Application ──► Job database (primary)\n                │                 │\n                │                 ├─► Standby for failure protection\n                │                 └─► Read replica for reporting\n                ▼\n              Job queue\n                ▼\n        Several AI workers / graphics processors<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Here, another question often matters more than the mere number of\ndatabase nodes: <strong>what happens if a worker finishes computing a\njob but does not receive the database response because of a\nfailover?<\/strong> The application then needs a clear model for job\nstates, acknowledgement and safe retries. We explore this further in\nM3-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monitoring – replication must be observed<\/h2>\n<p>An architecture becomes operable only when its actual state is\nmonitored. For replicated databases, this includes, for example:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Currency:<\/strong> how far behind is a standby in receiving\nand applying changes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Connection:<\/strong> is the replication channel established,\nand is the connection experiencing errors?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Roles:<\/strong> is there exactly the intended primary\nserver, and are the standbys assigned correctly?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Storage:<\/strong> are sufficient resources available for\ndata, change logs and temporary processing?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Failover readiness:<\/strong> can a standby actually take\nover, or does it exist in name only?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Connection switching:<\/strong> do the application interface,\nproxy and background workers know where new connections should go?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Security:<\/strong> are replication accounts, networks and\ndata transfers adequately protected?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recovery:<\/strong> are independently retained backups and\nsuccessful restoration tests available?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>PostgreSQL offers views of replication status, among other\nfacilities. In addition, <strong>replication slots<\/strong> can ensure\nthat WAL segments that are still needed are not removed prematurely.\nThis is useful but entails a risk of its own: if a replica falls behind\nfor a long time, retained log data may exhaust the primary server’s\nstorage space. Protective mechanisms must therefore also be monitored\nand bounded.<\/p>\n<p>Another reason not to rely blindly on a green status indicator is\nthat a test under normal conditions does not yet prove that an unplanned\nfailure under load or a network partition will be handled correctly.\nPlanned switchover tests, documented recovery procedures and clear\nresponsibilities are part of operations, not merely optional\nconveniences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What about multiple locations and ‘multi-region’?<\/h2>\n<p>Greater geographical distance between computers helps against certain\nlocal failures, but often increases latency for synchronous\nacknowledgements and may incur additional data transfer costs. Two\nservers in the same rack may both be affected by the failure of a shared\npower supply. Two servers in geographically distant regions are more\nindependent, but network interruptions become more relevant.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>failure domain<\/strong> is an area in which a shared fault\ncan affect several components, for example the same power supply,\nnetwork segment, availability zone or data centre. Redundancy is only as\ngood as the independence of its failure domains.<\/p>\n<p>An often appropriate progression is:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A single server with tested backups and a clear recovery\nstrategy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A primary server plus standby within a suitable environment, with\nprotected switching.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distributed standbys across independent failure domains, with\nadditional read replicas if needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Only where there is a genuine requirement, geographically extensive\nmulti-site operation, with careful analysis of latency, data-loss\nobjectives, network partitions and costs.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This is not a rigid ranking for every system but a guide to weighing\nincreasing complexity against a genuine need.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Risks, security and financial decisions<\/h2>\n<p>Additional database nodes mean additional operating systems or\nmanaged instances, storage devices, network traffic, certificates and\ncredentials. Operators must plan version compatibility, security\nupdates, replication permissions, backups, notifications and failure\nscenarios. It is particularly important that a replication stream can\ncontain sensitive data; access should therefore be explicitly restricted\nand the transfer appropriately protected over insecure networks.<\/p>\n<p>There are also <strong>common causes of failure<\/strong>: a faulty\napplication writes incorrect data on all nodes; an attacker may have\nexcessive privileges; a simultaneous infrastructure disruption affects\nseveral supposedly independent servers. A cluster is therefore not a\nuniversal remedy.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"simple-decision-guide\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simple decision guide<\/h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Goal / situation<\/th>\n<th>Often appropriate approach<\/th>\n<th>Additional effort<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Small application, short interruptions tolerable<\/td>\n<td>Single server, monitoring, backup, recovery plan<\/td>\n<td>Low to moderate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shorter interruption required in the event of server failure<\/td>\n<td>Primary + standby + controlled failover<\/td>\n<td>Monitoring, role logic, tests<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very many reporting and search queries<\/td>\n<td>One or more read replicas<\/td>\n<td>Query routing, dealing with lag<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acknowledged transactions should survive an individual node failure\nwith as little loss as possible<\/td>\n<td>Suitable synchronous replication plus correct failover<\/td>\n<td>Write latency, quorum/fencing, failure planning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stricter requirements concerning entire sites<\/td>\n<td>Redundancy across independent failure domains<\/td>\n<td>Network, latency, higher running costs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The examples describe <strong>architectural principles<\/strong>, not\ncurrent offers or pricing recommendations. A specific system requires an\nanalysis of its load profile, the importance of the data and the\nacceptable interruption time. The more components involved, the more\nopportunities there are for errors arising from misconfiguration or\ninsufficient maintenance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary – five key points<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Replication<\/strong> transfers data and changes between\nnodes; on its own it does not turn a database into an automatically\navailable service.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Asynchronous replication<\/strong> generally keeps writes\nindependent of standby acknowledgements but allows delays and potential\ndata loss during failover.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Synchronous replication<\/strong> ties acknowledgement to\ndefined feedback from other nodes; the resulting degree of safety\ndepends on acknowledgement levels and configuration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Read replicas<\/strong> can take on read tasks but do not\neliminate the single write primary. Outdated read results must be taken\ninto account.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>High availability and backups are different protective\nmeasures.<\/strong> Safe role decisions are needed against split-brain,\nand independent backups against erroneous deletions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"transition-to-m3-l3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transition to M3-L3<\/h3>\n<p>We have now considered <strong>multiple copies of the same\ndata<\/strong> on different computers. <strong>M3-L3 – Partitioning and\nsharding<\/strong> introduces a fundamentally different idea: not every\ncomputer receives everything; instead, different partitions of data are\ndeliberately distributed to different nodes. This can also distribute\nthe data volume and, under certain conditions, the write load, at the\ncost of additional coordination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Terms and abbreviations in this lesson<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>HA<\/td>\n<td><em>High availability<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary / Standby<\/td>\n<td>Write-leading primary server / replacement node following its\nstate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WAL<\/td>\n<td><em>Write-Ahead Log<\/em>, a log of changes written in advance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streaming Replication<\/td>\n<td>Continuous transfer of changes over a connection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Log Shipping<\/td>\n<td>Transfer of archived change-log segments<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read Replica<\/td>\n<td>Replica that can handle read requests<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replication Lag<\/td>\n<td>Delay between generated and applied changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failover / Switchover<\/td>\n<td>Unplanned transition to a replacement / planned role change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Split-Brain<\/td>\n<td>Two nodes simultaneously make conflicting claims to leadership<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fencing / STONITH<\/td>\n<td>Isolation of a no-longer-authorised instance / technical\nshutdown<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quorum<\/td>\n<td>Required number of acknowledgements from a group<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RPO<\/td>\n<td><em>Recovery Point Objective<\/em>, target for the acceptable\ndata-loss window<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RTO<\/td>\n<td><em>Recovery Time Objective<\/em>, target time to resume\noperation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read-Your-Writes<\/td>\n<td>See one’s own new changes in subsequent read queries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure Domain<\/td>\n<td>Area with a common cause of failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Researched and checked on 03.11.2026.<\/strong> Specific\nexamples refer to PostgreSQL 18 where product details are concerned.\nOther database systems may offer different methods and guarantees. No\nreal system was set up or tested for this lesson.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>High Availability, Load Balancing, and\nReplication<\/em>, overview and comparison of solutions: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/high-availability.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/high-availability.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Log-Shipping Standby Servers<\/em>, particularly\nstreaming, synchronicity, replication slots and cascading replication:\n<a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Failover<\/em>, promotion, handling the old\nprimary and split-brain: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby-failover.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby-failover.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Logical Replication<\/em>, publish–subscribe and\ndistinction from physical replication: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Restrictions of Logical Replication<\/em>, schema\nand sequence limitations: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Backup and Restore<\/em>, independent backups and\ncontinuous archiving: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/backup.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/backup.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Replication Configuration<\/em>, synchronous\nstandby selection and quorum variants: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-replication.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-replication.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Scope of the lesson:<\/strong> the figures and times in\nexamples are freely chosen illustrations, not measured or guaranteed\ndelays. M3-L3 explains partitioning and sharding, M3-L4 other database\nsystems, M3-L5 distributed correctness and M3-L6 backups/recovery in\ndetail.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Partitioning and sharding<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initial question: Why is a second copy not always\nsufficient?<\/h2>\n<p>In M3-L1, we examined how a single database server manages tables,\nindexes, transactions and concurrent queries. In M3-L2, we learnt how to\ntransfer <strong>copies of the same data<\/strong> to other servers. This\ncan help during failures and distribute read queries. Replication,\nhowever, does not solve every problem of scale.<\/p>\n<p>Imagine an online service whose dataset grows over several years. It\nmight contain customer data, jobs and analysis results. Eventually,\nstorage on a single server becomes scarce. Or so many concurrent changes\narrive that even an upgraded primary server becomes a bottleneck.\nFurther read replicas then help only to a limited extent: the write\nworkload is still concentrated on the primary server.<\/p>\n<p>Another strategy is that <strong>not all the data should reside on\nevery computer<\/strong>. Instead, we divide the dataset into carefully\nchosen sections. In a simple variant, these sections remain within one\ndatabase instance. In a more advanced variant, the parts are distributed\nacross <strong>different database nodes<\/strong>. The terms for these\nconcepts are <em>partitioning<\/em> and <em>sharding<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>Initially, this sounds like a simple distribution task. The crucial\nquestions only arise afterwards: where is a specific customer? What\nhappens if their order is on another server? How do we calculate a grand\ntotal? How do we change the distribution if a server is overloaded? And\nhow do we prevent inconsistent data in the event of a failure?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding idea of this lesson:<\/strong> partitioning is\nprimarily a way of organising data logically and physically; sharding is\na form of horizontal distribution across separate data storage units or\nnodes. The gain in storage capacity and potentially write capacity comes\nat the cost of more complex coordination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>First, the distinction: Partition, shard and replica<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_019_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison: replication copies data to replicas, while sharding distributes subsets of data across nodes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison: replication copies data to\nreplicas, while sharding distributes subsets of data across\nnodes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simplified comparison: replicas hold copies of the data;\nshards hold different subsets. Replication and sharding can be combined,\nand both require additional measures for availability and fault\ntolerance.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A <strong>partition<\/strong> is part of a larger dataset. For\nexample, the entries in a single logical table <code>Bestellungen<\/code>\n(orders) can be stored in separate physical sections according to their\norder month. The table may still appear to an application as a single\ntable.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>shard<\/strong> is a subset of data that is managed\nindependently on a database instance or database node in the context of\ndistributed database systems. A shard is often itself a collection of\nrelated table rows. A physical server may host several shards; a shard\nis therefore <strong>not necessarily equivalent to an entire\nserver<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>replica<\/strong>, in turn, is an additional copy of data\nalready held elsewhere. Sharding and replication can be combined: each\nshard can itself be replicated.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-three-different-ideas\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nThree different ideas<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1) Table partitioning on ONE database server\n\n             Logical table ‘Orders’\n                      │\n          ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n          ▼           ▼           ▼\n       January     February     March\n          └───────────┴───────────┘\n              One database server\n\n2) Sharding across MULTIPLE database nodes\n\n          All customers and orders\n                      │\n                   Routing\n                      │\n          ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n          ▼           ▼           ▼\n       Shard A      Shard B      Shard C\n       Server 1     Server 2     Server 3\n\n3) Replication of a shard\n\n          Shard A (leading copy)\n                      │\n                 Copy changes\n                      ▼\n          Shard A (second copy)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In the first case, the organisation may help with maintenance and\ncertain queries, but the hardware remains the same. In the second case,\ndata and work are distributed across several nodes. In the third case,\nthe same subset exists redundantly. The terms describe different\nproperties and must not be treated as equivalent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What do horizontal and vertical partitioning mean?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Horizontal partitioning<\/strong> divides the <em>rows<\/em> of\na table. The sections generally have comparable columns but contain\ndifferent records.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customers (one logical table)\n\nID    Name       Country\n10    Ada        DE\n11    Ben        FR\n12    Cem        DE\n13    Dia        IT\n\nPartition A: ID 10 and 11\nPartition B: ID 12 and 13<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Vertical partitioning<\/strong>, by contrast, separates\n<em>columns<\/em> or functional data areas. Frequently needed customer\nnames and account balances, for instance, could be kept separately from\nlarge profile information that is rarely read. This can avoid\nunnecessary data access but may also require additional joins.<\/p>\n<p>In everyday usage, whole functional areas are also distributed to\ntheir own databases, for example accounting and media management. This\nis more of a <strong>functional separation<\/strong> of systems, not\nautomatically the sharding of a shared table.<\/p>\n<p>In the following sections, we mainly consider horizontal\npartitioning. It allows a dataset to be divided into subsets according\nto a key and, where appropriate, distributed across several\ncomputers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Partitioned tables in a single PostgreSQL database<\/h2>\n<p>PostgreSQL 18 supports <strong>declarative table\npartitioning<\/strong>: a parent, logically unified table is described,\nand a partition key specifies how the data is distributed across\nphysical child tables. Range, list and hash partitioning are examples of\nthe available methods.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a shop’s orders. One partition contains January orders, the\nnext February orders and the third March orders. A query for February\ncan then often skip the other months. This optimisation is called\n<strong>partition pruning<\/strong>, meaning the exclusion of irrelevant\npartitions.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Query: ‘Orders in February’\n                 │\n                 ▼\n        PostgreSQL query planner\n                 │\n       ┌─────────┼──────────┐\n       │         ▼          │\n    January   February    March\n      skip      search     skip<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Partitioning can also simplify the removal of entire old time\nperiods. Instead of deleting millions of individual rows, a suitable old\npartition can be detached or removed. Whether this can be done safely\ndepends on the retention rule and the overall data model.<\/p>\n<p>The limitations matter: not every query becomes faster. A query\nwithout an appropriate filter may have to examine all partitions. Too\nmany small partitions may complicate planning and management. And a\ntable partitioned on one server is <strong>not automatically distributed\nacross multiple servers<\/strong> as a result.<\/p>\n<p>A specific PostgreSQL characteristic shows that partitioning is more\nthan a cosmetic division: on a partitioned table, a global\n<code>PRIMARY KEY<\/code> or <code>UNIQUE<\/code> constraint must include\nthe partition key columns (subject to the other applicable\nrequirements). Independent local indexes cannot simply establish\nuniqueness across arbitrary partitions on their own. A data model that\npreviously used a single unique key without difficulty may therefore\nrequire adjustment when it is partitioned.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> Large logs, event or measurement\ntables organised by time, frequent time-range queries and regular data\narchiving. <strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong> Small tables, freely\ncombined search conditions unrelated to the partition key, or a system\nthat is already fast enough with a suitable index.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The basic problem in sharding: Who knows where a row\nis?<\/h2>\n<p>With a single database server, an application does not usually have\nto know which disk holds a record. The database server organises access.\nWith sharding, this is insufficient: some component must decide\n<strong>which shard is responsible<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>This may be part of the application, a routing layer in front of it\nor a database system that largely manages the distribution itself. The\n<strong>shard key<\/strong>, or distribution key, determines which subset\na record belongs to. This is often a stable customer number or tenant\nidentifier.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>      Web application: customer 2847 loads their jobs\n                            │\n                            ▼\n                        Shard router\n                     Key: customer 2847\n                            │\n              ‘Customer 2847 is on shard B’\n                            │\n                            ▼\n                        Database B\n                   Customer + their jobs<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A well-chosen key enables targeted access. A poorly chosen key\ndistributes data unevenly, produces unnecessary queries across several\nservers or makes subsequent changes expensive. The shard key should\ntherefore be chosen according to <strong>actual read and write\npatterns<\/strong>, not an arbitrary existing column.<\/p>\n<p>A single database node can contain several shards. This makes it\neasier to move smaller units when necessary instead of splitting a huge\ndatabase each time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four important distribution strategies<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"61-bereichsbasierte-verteilung-range-sharding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Range-based\ndistribution (range sharding)<\/h3>\n<p>The value range of the key is divided into sections. For example,\ncustomer numbers from 1 to 9999 reside on shard A, and 10000 to 19999 on\nshard B. This is easy to understand and is suitable when <em>contiguous\nranges<\/em> are queried frequently.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ID 00001–09999  ──► Shard A\nCustomer ID 10000–19999  ──► Shard B\nCustomer ID 20000–29999  ──► Shard C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Problem:<\/strong> if new customers are particularly active\nand receive steadily increasing numbers, the new write load often\nconcentrates on the latest range. A large customer can also generate\nsignificantly more records than many small customers combined.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"62-hashbasierte-verteilung-hash-sharding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hash-based\ndistribution (hash sharding)<\/h3>\n<p>Here, the key is converted using a <strong>hash function<\/strong>\ninto a numerical value that can be used for distribution. A hash\nfunction produces another value from an input according to fixed rules.\nBased on this value, the system decides which section the row belongs\nto.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer 2847 ──► Hash function ──► Range B ──► Shard B\nCustomer 2848 ──► Hash function ──► Range A ──► Shard A\nCustomer 2849 ──► Hash function ──► Range C ──► Shard C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The advantage is often a more even spread of consecutive customer\nnumbers. The disadvantage is that contiguous customer numbers are now\nunlikely to be located together. Range queries may have to visit many\nshards. A simple scheme such as\n<code>Hashwert modulo Anzahl Server<\/code> (hash value modulo number of\nservers) also makes adding new servers difficult: if the number of\nservers changes, many keys can change their assignment. Real systems\ntherefore often use additional layers of abstraction such as virtual\nranges.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"63-verzeichnissystem-oder-lookup-sharding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Directory-based or\nlookup sharding<\/h3>\n<p>A mapping table records which customer is located on which shard. The\nrouter queries this table and forwards the request. This allows targeted\nchanges: a particularly large customer can move to a dedicated shard\nwhile the remaining customers stay where they are.<\/p>\n<p>The cost is additional metadata management. The directory must be\navailable, up to date and consistent. An incorrect entry leads to the\nwrong database node.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"64-geografische-verteilung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Geographical distribution<\/h3>\n<p>Data can be divided by region, for example when accesses from one\nregion should mainly be handled there. This can shorten communication\npaths or accommodate special storage-location requirements. At the same\ntime, other difficulties arise: one region may be used much more heavily\nthan another; users travel; and data needed worldwide must remain\naccessible across regional boundaries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> geographical storage location alone does\nnot determine the legal permissibility of data processing. Access\nrights, provider roles, transfers and further requirements must be\nexamined separately.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"comparison\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison<\/h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy<\/th>\n<th>Strength<\/th>\n<th>Typical risk<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranges<\/td>\n<td>Related values remain close together, simple range queries<\/td>\n<td>Hot latest range, unequal sizes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hash<\/td>\n<td>Often a better spread<\/td>\n<td>Expensive range queries, redistribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Directory<\/td>\n<td>Flexible assignment of individual customers<\/td>\n<td>Metadata, additional routing dependency<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geography<\/td>\n<td>Regional proximity to data<\/td>\n<td>Regional hotspots, global access<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Hybrid forms exist. For instance, the region can first determine the\ndata location, and a hash function can distribute customers within that\nregion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A running example: Customers and orders<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a software-as-a-service offering (<strong>SaaS<\/strong>:\nsoftware provided as an online service) with many business customers.\nFor each customer, there are users, orders and billing data. A typical\nrequest is: ‘Show me the last 20 jobs from customer 2847.’<\/p>\n<p>If <strong>customers, users and jobs belonging to the same\ntenant<\/strong> reside together on one shard, the database can handle\nthis request locally. This is the principle of\n<strong>co-location<\/strong>: data that is frequently queried together\nis organised according to the same distribution key.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                 Shard router\n                      │\n          ┌───────────┴──────────┐\n          │                      │\n          ▼                      ▼\n       Shard A                Shard B\n       Customer 101           Customer 2847\n       Users 101/*            Users 2847/*\n       Jobs 101/*             Jobs 2847/*\n          │                      │\n       Local JOINs            Local JOINs<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>JOIN<\/strong> is the relational combination of records from\ndifferent tables using corresponding keys. Locally, this is relatively\nsimple: a database server knows the participating tables and indexes.\nAcross different nodes, it becomes harder because data has to be\ntransferred over the network or subqueries have to run on multiple\ncomputers.<\/p>\n<p>Note that <strong>not every system provides this co-location\nautomatically<\/strong>. An application must maintain the keys correctly\nin its data model, or a distributed database system must configure the\ntables accordingly. Strict separation of customer data is also a\nseparate security task: sharding by customer identifier <strong>does not\nreplace authorisation checks<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why cross-shard JOINs can become expensive<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose that the <code>Kunden<\/code> (customers) table is on server A\nand the corresponding <code>Bestellungen<\/code> (orders) table is on\nserver B. A query that combines all customers with their latest orders\ncan no longer simply operate on one storage device. The required rows\nmust come together somewhere.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>           Query: customers with order value\n                         │\n                     Coordinator\n                     /         \\\n                    ▼           ▼\n                Server A      Server B\n                Customers     Orders\n                    \\           /\n                     ▼         ▼\n                      Merge data\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                       Result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Every data transfer increases execution time and network load.\nSubqueries may have different execution times. If a participating shard\nfails, the entire query can fail. Sometimes it is cheaper to copy a\nsmall, rarely changed <strong>reference table<\/strong>, such as country\ncodes, to several nodes. This avoids remote lookups but requires\ncontrolled updates to these copies.<\/p>\n<p>A particularly problematic case is <strong>fan-out queries<\/strong>:\na query cannot be sent to a specific shard and has to be distributed\nacross all shards. Each node returns a partial result, and the\ncoordinator combines or sorts them. This can be useful for an overall\nstatistic. For every individual user interaction, it is often\nunnecessarily expensive.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Key point:<\/strong> a good sharding strategy is guided by the\n<em>most frequent queries<\/em>, not merely by achieving an equal number\nof stored rows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Searching, counting and sorting across many shards<\/h2>\n<p>Individual customer searches are simple in the above model. But what\nif management wants to know the combined revenue from all customers?<\/p>\n<p>Each shard can first calculate a local subtotal. The coordinator then\nadds the results. This principle is called\n<strong>scatter/gather<\/strong>: distribute work and collect partial\nresults.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>               ‘Revenue from all customers’\n                         │\n                     Coordinator\n                ┌────────┼────────┐\n                ▼        ▼        ▼\n             Shard A   Shard B   Shard C\n             12,000     7,000     9,000\n                └────────┼────────┘\n                         ▼\n                     Total 28,000<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The figures are purely illustrative. Addition is easy to break down\nin this way. Other operations are more complicated: to find the <em>ten\nlargest orders worldwide<\/em>, local top lists and then a correct global\nmerge may be necessary. With complex filters and nested joins, the\nvolume of data transferred between nodes can grow substantially.<\/p>\n<p>Some systems therefore separate ongoing user transactions from\nexpensive analytics. Data may, for example, be transferred to a separate\nanalytics environment after a delay. This is, however, a new copy with\npossible delays in freshness, not inherently an always-consistent live\nstate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Writes and transactions across multiple shards<\/h2>\n<p>A write operation that affects only data on a single shard can often\nbe handled as a normal transaction by its local database. An operation\nthat must change <strong>two shards jointly<\/strong> is more\ndifficult.<\/p>\n<p>Example: a balance is to be transferred from customer account A to\ncustomer account B. Account A is on shard 1, and account B is on shard\n2. The amount must not disappear on one side and never appear on the\nother.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                ‘Transfer 50 units’\n                         │\n                  Transaction logic\n                    /           \\\n                   ▼             ▼\n                Shard 1        Shard 2\n                Debit A        Credit B\n                    \\           /\n                  Correct together?<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>local ACID transaction<\/strong> (Atomicity, Consistency,\nIsolation, Durability) on shard 1 does not automatically guarantee\ncomplete execution on shard 2. Some distributed database systems support\ncoordinated transactions with defined guarantees. Other architectures\nuse separate bookings, events, retry logic and compensating steps. Which\nvariant is acceptable for the business process depends strongly on the\nuse case.<\/p>\n<p>The classic <strong>two-phase commit<\/strong> (2PC) can involve\nmultiple participants in a common decision: first prepare, then commit\nor roll back together. However, this increases network communication and\ncreates new failure scenarios. We examine the precise interaction with\nfailures, locks and recovery in <strong>M3-L5 – Transactions and\ndistributed correctness<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Not every distributed system forgoes strict consistency. And not\nevery system supporting sharding provides the same transaction\nguarantees. <strong>Sharding describes data distribution, not\nautomatically the consistency model.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Unique identifiers and foreign keys<\/h2>\n<p>In a single database, a unique order number can easily be checked by\na central index. When orders are distributed across multiple shards, the\nquestion arises: who prevents two nodes from assigning the same number\nsimultaneously?<\/p>\n<p>A common strategy is to design identifiers so that they are\nsufficiently unique without a central sequential numbering service, for\nexample by using suitable composite identifiers or distributed ID\nschemes. Another option is to use a shared allocation service. Both\napproaches have their own advantages and disadvantages regarding\ncomplexity, availability and coordination requirements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Foreign keys<\/strong>, which are rules such as requiring an\norder to belong to an existing customer, cannot be enforced as easily\nacross server boundaries in every system as they can within a local\ndatabase. When customers and their orders are located together, the\ncheck is significantly easier. Globally distributed relationships need\nadditional database capabilities or application-level checks.<\/p>\n<p>This does not mean that relational rules are inherently impossible in\ndistributed databases. It means that the guarantees are <strong>a\nspecific system property<\/strong> that needs to be checked.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hotspots: When one shard slows everything down<\/h2>\n<p>Even data volume is not the same as even load. Perhaps ten small\nbusiness customers together have as many records as one large customer.\nHowever, the large customer sends a thousand times as many queries. Its\nshard then remains under continuous load while other nodes do little\nwork.<\/p>\n<p>This is called a <strong>hotspot<\/strong>, a subset of data under\ndisproportionately heavy load. Hotspots can arise because of:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">a very large or very active tenant;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">a monotonically increasing key that concentrates new writes in the\nlatest range;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">a particularly frequently queried object or a global counter\ntable;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">unevenly distributed time periods, regions or job types;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">poor query plans or missing local indexes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Additional servers do not help if all hot writes continue to land on\n<strong>the same single shard<\/strong>. Possible countermeasures include\na different distribution key, splitting an oversized shard, moving a\nlarge customer, a targeted cache for readers or redesigning write\noperations at the business-logic level.<\/p>\n<p>All these changes can be expensive. A sharding design should\ntherefore consider not only today’s average but also realistic peaks and\nparticularly large customers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rebalancing: What happens when a fourth server is\nadded?<\/h2>\n<p>When a cluster grows, switching on a new computer is not enough.\nInitially, the new computer has no data and therefore no useful work.\nParts of the existing dataset must move to it. This process is called\n<strong>rebalancing<\/strong>, meaning the redistribution of data and\nload to restore balance.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Before:\n  Server A: Shard 1, 2, 3\n  Server B: Shard 4, 5, 6\n  Server C: Shard 7, 8, 9\n\nAfter adding server D (example only):\n  Server A: Shard 1, 2\n  Server B: Shard 4, 5\n  Server C: Shard 7, 8\n  Server D: Shard 3, 6, 9<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>During the move, a record must not be modified independently on both\nnodes without control. Depending on the product, migration may\ntemporarily restrict writes, replay changes and finally switch the\nrouting metadata. This places additional load on the network, storage\ndevices and processor. A move must be fault-tolerant: what happens if a\nconnection fails during copying?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Virtual shards<\/strong> or many small sections often make\nthis work more flexible. Instead of redistributing all customer data,\nonly certain sections are moved between physical servers. How\nautomatically this happens depends on the product.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mongodb-as-a-concrete-example\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>MongoDB as a concrete\nexample<\/h3>\n<p>MongoDB distributes documents in a <em>sharded collection<\/em> across\nmultiple shards using a shard key. Its documented architecture uses\n<strong>chunks<\/strong>, sections of the key space, and a\n<strong>balancer<\/strong> that can move suitable ranges between shards.\nMongoDB supports both range-based and hash-based strategies. This is a\nconcrete product example, not the general definition of all distributed\ndatabases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Routing and the coordinator: Who becomes the new\nbottleneck?<\/h2>\n<p>A distributed system needs a controlled answer to the question of\nwhich node is responsible for which data. The router may be integrated\ninto the application or operate as a separate component. A distributed\ndatabase system may also handle this task itself.<\/p>\n<p>For example, the documentation of the PostgreSQL extension\n<strong>Citus<\/strong> describes an architecture with coordinator and\nworker nodes. The coordinator knows distribution metadata and can\nforward queries to a specific worker or parallelise them across multiple\nworkers. The actual table rows are distributed across the workers.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                Application / SQL query\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                  Coordinator / router\n                   /        |        \\\n                  ▼         ▼         ▼\n              Worker A  Worker B  Worker C\n               Shards     Shards    Shards<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>SQL<\/strong> stands for <em>Structured Query Language<\/em>.\nUsing SQL does not mean that every distributed query can be executed as\ncheaply as on a local database. For complex queries, the coordinator has\nto combine partial results and may itself become a load bottleneck or\npoint of failure. Such systems therefore need suitable operation of\ntheir control and metadata layer.<\/p>\n<p>The product distinction is important: <strong>built-in PostgreSQL\ntable partitioning does not by itself distribute data across independent\ndatabase servers as a full-fledged sharding system<\/strong>. Citus adds\nseparate distributed features. MongoDB uses a different architecture.\nFurther distributed SQL systems are compared in M3-L4.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Considering sharding and replication together<\/h2>\n<p>In a serious multi-node architecture, each shard can have its own\nredundant dataset. Instead of operating three independent database\nservers, one may then operate several groups of primary and replacement\nnodes.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                   Applications\n                        │\n                   Shard router\n                 /      |      \\\n                ▼       ▼       ▼\n             Shard A  Shard B  Shard C\n             Primary  Primary  Primary\n                │       │       │\n                ▼       ▼       ▼\n             Replica A Replica B Replica C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Here, <strong>sharding<\/strong> meets the objective of dividing the\ndata. <strong>Replication<\/strong> meets a different objective: keeping\ncopies of the respective parts. Actual availability also depends on\nfailover, networks, quorum rules and application behaviour.<\/p>\n<p>A failure of shard B might leave customers on A and C unaffected –\n<strong>provided that<\/strong> their queries are genuinely independent.\nIf the home page needs global statistics from all three shards, the\nfailure can still affect the entire user experience. Physically\nseparating the data alone is therefore not sufficient. The application\nmust have suitable fault boundaries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operations: Monitoring, updates, backups and data\nprotection<\/h2>\n<p>Sharding increases the operational footprint of a system. Every\nadditional node needs storage devices, memory, security updates,\nsuitable access controls, monitoring and recovery procedures. Routers,\nmetadata, network connections and possibly replicas are added to\nthis.<\/p>\n<p>It is not only CPU and memory that must be monitored, but also the\n<strong>distribution<\/strong>: how large are the individual shards? How\nmany reads and writes reach them? Are there particularly slow nodes? How\nlong do moves take? Do subqueries get stuck on a failed shard? Can\nqueries be routed to specific shards, or do they cause unnecessary\nfan-outs?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backups<\/strong> become more complicated. Every shard must be\nbacked up, but for related business processes, the <em>common point in\ntime<\/em> may also be crucial. If shard A is restored to Monday and\nshard B to Wednesday, relationships across shards can become\ninconsistent. Backups and their restoration should therefore be planned\nfor the entire system and actually tested. Replicas do not replace these\nbackups.<\/p>\n<p>When the database schema changes, several shards must reach mutually\ncompatible states. Data migrations may move sensitive records over\nadditional network connections. Access must be authenticated, authorised\nand encrypted where necessary; logs should not contain unnecessary\npersonal information. Multi-tenancy also requires that even an\nincorrectly routed request must not lead to another customer’s data.<\/p>\n<p>All of this generates costs: additional database instances, storage\ncopies, data transfer, backups, monitoring and, above all, operational\ntime. There is therefore no general rule such as ‘three smaller shards\nare cheaper than one large database server’. Actual pricing decisions\nwould require current provider prices and a measured load profile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three realistic application scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"a-lokaler-medienkatalog\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A. Local media catalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local tool manages hundreds of thousands of media files and\nmetadata. Searches for file names, tags or project membership run on a\nsingle computer. <strong>Sharding would usually be an unnecessary\nadditional source of errors here.<\/strong> Suitable SQLite indexes,\nfull-text search if required, good query planning and tested backups are\nmore useful. Even if the data is organised by year, this need not turn\ninto a multi-server system.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"b-bezahlter-online-analysedienst-fuer-viele-kunden\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>B. Paid\nonline analysis service for many customers<\/h3>\n<p>An online offering stores the results of different customers. If a\nsingle database can no longer handle the actual load profile, the\ncustomer number could serve as the shard key. A customer account and its\nanalysis jobs would then reside together on one node. A small customer\ncould share a shard with many others; a very large customer may need a\ndedicated area. Global business statistics must be explicitly considered\nas cross-shard queries.<\/p>\n<p>The operational question is not just ‘How many customers do I have?’\nbut ‘How much data, how many concurrent accesses, how many changes per\ncustomer and how critical are failures?’ A small service with a few\nhundred inactive or low-activity customers may not need sharding at\nall.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"c-ki-verarbeitungsdienst-mit-hintergrundauftraegen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>C. AI\nprocessing service with background jobs<\/h3>\n<p>A cloud application accepts image or text processing jobs. Several\n<strong>workers<\/strong>, independent background processes, handle them.\nLarge files may reside in separate object storage. A database only holds\njob status, customer identifiers, references to results and billing\nentries.<\/p>\n<p>Here the <strong>computation<\/strong> is often the initial\nbottleneck, not the metadata database. Additional workers can be started\nwithout sharding the database. Only when the central job or event table\ndemonstrably reaches its limits does the question of partitioning or\nusing multiple database nodes arise. A job register partitioned by\ntenant may make sense; a global priority queue, by contrast, requires\nspecial coordination and is not automatically solved by sharding.<\/p>\n<p>These examples show that <strong>more processing computers, more\ndatabase replicas and more shards solve three different\nproblems.<\/strong> The infrastructure should be selected according to\nthe actual bottleneck.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide: When to use partitioning, and when\nsharding?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Initial situation<\/th>\n<th>Examine first<\/th>\n<th>Why not shard immediately?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Small to medium-sized application<\/td>\n<td>One server, well-designed indexes and queries<\/td>\n<td>Simple operations and local transactions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very large time-series table<\/td>\n<td>Table partitioning by time<\/td>\n<td>Data remains manageable in one instance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many read queries, moderate write load<\/td>\n<td>Caching and read replicas<\/td>\n<td>No new distributed write path needed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insufficient storage or write capacity on one node<\/td>\n<td>Suitable shard distribution, measured load<\/td>\n<td>More nodes are useful only with good routing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Largest load originates from one customer<\/td>\n<td>Examine tenant isolation or repartitioning<\/td>\n<td>Merely adding other shards does not resolve the hotspot<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many global JOINs and atomic operations<\/td>\n<td>Review data model and transaction requirements<\/td>\n<td>Sharding could make the dominant case worse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A useful sequence is to <strong>measure first, then improve queries\nand indexes, subsequently partition tables or distribute reads where\nappropriate, and distribute data that is written across multiple nodes\nonly when genuinely needed.<\/strong> This sequence is a rule of thumb,\nnot a mandatory ranking for every system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary – six key points<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Table partitioning<\/strong> organises a logical dataset and\ncan take place on a single server.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sharding<\/strong> distributes subsets across separate\ndatabase units or nodes; a shard need not correspond to a server.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A good <strong>shard key<\/strong> keeps data that is frequently\nneeded together in the same location and enables targeted routing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Cross-shard JOINs, global analytics and distributed\ntransactions<\/strong> make operation more complex and often slower.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hotspots and rebalancing<\/strong> remain important problems\neven with many servers; equally sized shards do not automatically mean\neven load.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sharding, replication and backup<\/strong> are independent\nconcepts that complement but do not replace one another.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"transition-to-m3-l4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transition to M3-L4<\/h3>\n<p>Today we have learnt the <strong>basic mechanics of\ndistribution<\/strong>. In <strong>M3-L4 – Distributed SQL / NoSQL /\nOracle / PostgreSQL<\/strong>, we compare systems that implement these\nmechanisms in different ways. We examine which data-distribution\ncapabilities are already part of the database system, how relational\nguarantees are implemented and what compromises arise regarding\nconsistency and availability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Terms and abbreviations in this lesson<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Partitioning<\/td>\n<td>Division of a dataset into physical/logical sections<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal partitioning<\/td>\n<td>Distribute different rows across different sections<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical partitioning<\/td>\n<td>Keep columns or data areas separate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shard<\/td>\n<td>Independently managed subset of a distributed dataset<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shard key<\/td>\n<td>Distribution key that helps determine the assignment of a\nrecord<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shard router<\/td>\n<td>Routing component that directs queries to the responsible\nshards<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partition pruning<\/td>\n<td>Skip unnecessary table partitions during a query<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Co-location<\/td>\n<td>Deliberately store data frequently needed together in the same\nlocation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JOIN<\/td>\n<td>Relational combination of multiple tables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fan-out / scatter-gather<\/td>\n<td>Distribute a query to several nodes / collect partial results<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hotspot<\/td>\n<td>Section or node under excessively heavy load<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rebalancing<\/td>\n<td>Redistribute shards or sections when load changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference table<\/td>\n<td>Small dataset frequently needed together with other data, possibly\ncopied to nodes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ACID<\/td>\n<td>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability: transaction\nproperties<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2PC<\/td>\n<td>Two-phase commit, a two-phase protocol for distributed\ncommitment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SaaS<\/td>\n<td>Software as a Service, software provided online<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL<\/td>\n<td>Structured Query Language, structured database query language<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worker<\/td>\n<td>Background process for independently executable tasks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Primary and project documentation, checked on\n03.11.2026.<\/strong> The product examples explain the capabilities of\nspecific systems; they do not constitute a general guarantee, pricing\nresearch or a practical installation test.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Table Partitioning<\/em> – declarative\npartitioning, range/list/hash, partition pruning and constraints: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Microsoft Learn, <em>Sharding pattern<\/em> –\nrange/hash/lookup/geography, key selection, routing, cross-shard\nqueries, rebalancing and operational risks: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/patterns/sharding\">https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/patterns/sharding<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">MongoDB, <em>Sharding<\/em> – shard keys, chunks, balancer,\nrange-based and hash-based strategies: <a\nhref=\"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sharding/\">https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sharding/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">MongoDB, <em>Choose a Shard Key<\/em> – cardinality, distribution and\nhotspots: <a\nhref=\"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key/\">https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Citus, <em>Concepts<\/em> – PostgreSQL extension, coordinator/worker,\ndistributed tables and co-location: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.citusdata.com/en/stable/get_started/concepts.html\">https://docs.citusdata.com/en/stable/get_started/concepts.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Source limitations:<\/strong> the sources describe concepts\nand specific product features. The customer numbers, order amounts,\nserver counts and example architectures in this lesson are freely\nconstructed. Whether a system is suitable for an actual service can only\nbe determined from requirements, documented guarantees and\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Distributed SQL, NoSQL, Oracle and PostgreSQL<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initial question: What kind of database do we need for many\nservers?<\/h2>\n<p>In M3-L1, we became familiar with a single database instance. In\nM3-L2, we examined copies of the same database that are useful for read\naccess or fault tolerance. In M3-L3, we divided datasets into individual\nsections called <strong>shards<\/strong>. The question now is:\n<strong>which of these tasks does each database system already handle\nitself?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A small PHP application can run with an SQLite file. For a larger web\nservice, one might use PostgreSQL or another server-based database.\nLater, multiple locations, rapidly increasing write load or stricter\nfault-tolerance requirements may become relevant. It is therefore\ntempting to look for a ‘distributed database’. But this expression does\nnot identify a single technology; it describes several fundamentally\ndifferent architectures.<\/p>\n<p>A database can, for example, <em>replicate<\/em> the same information\nacross multiple servers. It can <em>distribute<\/em> its data across\nmultiple servers. It may allow several instances to access\n<strong>shared storage<\/strong> in parallel. Or it may hide the\ndistribution behind an SQL interface and perform complex coordination\nfor transactions. Such systems solve different problems and have\ndifferent limitations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding idea:<\/strong> the most important question is not\ninitially ‘SQL or NoSQL?’ but: what data relationships and correctness\nguarantees does my application need, what load actually arises, and what\noperational responsibility can I handle?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Keeping the terms clearly distinct<\/h2>\n<p><strong>SQL<\/strong> stands for <em>Structured Query Language<\/em>.\nIt is used, among other things, to query or modify data in relational\ndatabases. <strong>An SQL query is not a database server.<\/strong><\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_020_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison of a single instance, replication and partitioning/sharding\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A single instance holds one data store, replication\ncreates copies, and sharding partitions records across database\nnodes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A <strong>relational database management system (RDBMS)<\/strong>\nstores data in logically related tables and provides, among other\nthings, keys, relationships and transactions. PostgreSQL and Oracle\nDatabase belong to this family. The word ‘relational’ describes the data\nmodel, not automatically the number of servers involved.<\/p>\n<p><strong>NoSQL<\/strong> is an umbrella term for database systems that\ndo not primarily follow the classical relational SQL model or that also\nemphasise other models. It does not mean that the data must be\n‘unstructured’, that transactions are impossible or that consistency is\ninherently weak. The properties vary considerably between products and\nsettings.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Distributed SQL<\/strong> describes systems that combine an\nSQL interface and essential relational properties with data storage\ndistributed across multiple nodes. These systems attempt to organise\nsharding, replication, query planning and transaction coordination\nlargely within the database system. However, individual products differ\nregarding SQL compatibility, isolation levels, consistency guarantees\nand operational models.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-the-same-application-three-different-approaches-to-data-storage\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nThe same application, three different approaches to data storage<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>A) One relational database server\n\n   PHP application ─── SQL ───&gt; PostgreSQL server\n                                     │\n                                 All tables\n\nB) Replicated relational database\n\n                           ┌──&gt; Standby (copy)\n   PHP application ───&gt; Primary server\n                           └──&gt; Standby (copy)\n\nC) Distributed SQL database (schematic)\n\n   PHP application ─── SQL ───&gt; SQL access / coordinator\n                                         │\n                                  Plan and route query\n                                         │\n                              ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n                              ▼          ▼          ▼\n                            Node A     Node B     Node C\n                            Subset     Subset     Subset\n                            + copies   + copies   + copies<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The three diagrams do not constitute a ranking of quality. The\nsimplest solution can be the right one technically and economically. In\nthe third architecture, substantial coordination is needed in addition\nto the actual database work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Relational tables, documents and key-value stores<\/h2>\n<p>Choosing a database begins with the structure of the problem.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"31-relationale-datenbanken\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Relational databases<\/h3>\n<p>An online service may have tables for <code>Kunden<\/code>\n(customers), <code>Aufträge<\/code> (jobs) and <code>Zahlungen<\/code>\n(payments). A job has a customer number that can be used to identify the\ncorresponding customer. Such relationships can be evaluated using a\n<strong>JOIN<\/strong> (a combination of tables). Primary and foreign\nkeys can help prevent invalid relationships.<\/p>\n<p>The challenge is that when data is distributed, data from different\ntables or nodes may have to be read and changed together. Relational\nfunctions do not disappear, but their implementation becomes more\ndifficult.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"32-dokumentenorientierte-datenbanken\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Document-oriented\ndatabases<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>document database<\/strong> such as MongoDB stores related\ndata in documents, often in a JSON-like structure. JSON stands for\n<em>JavaScript Object Notation<\/em>, a widely used text format for\nstructured data. A customer document might contain several addresses in\naddition to a name and identifier.<\/p>\n<p>This can simplify an application when an entire object is usually\nread or modified. If many objects are frequently linked to one another,\nhowever, duplicated information, special query methods and update rules\ncan create additional work. MongoDB now also supports multi-document\ntransactions; the ‘document’ model therefore certainly does not mean ‘no\ntransactions’.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"33-schluessel-wert-datenbanken\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Key-value databases<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>key-value store<\/strong> associates a value with a unique\nkey:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>&quot;auftrag:4812:status&quot;  →  &quot;fertig&quot;\n&quot;nutzer:102:ansicht&quot;   →  &quot;kompakt&quot;<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is attractive for simple and frequent accesses. Such a model is\nnot automatically suitable for complex relationships and analytics. Some\nkey-value systems are particularly often used as caches; others are\npersistent database systems.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"34-weitere-nosql-familien\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further NoSQL families<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Wide-column systems<\/strong> often organise large amounts of\ndata by partition keys and column-oriented or column-family-like\nstructures. <strong>Graph databases<\/strong> primarily describe\nrelationships between nodes and edges. Both solve specialised problems\nbut do not inherently replace every relational database.<\/p>\n<p>The label ‘NoSQL’ therefore says nothing by itself about actual\navailability, distribution or consistency. These properties must be\nchecked for the specific product and operating mode chosen.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distributed SQL: A database that coordinates many nodes\ninternally<\/h2>\n<p>The goal of a distributed SQL database can be understood as\n<strong>one logical database system with several physical\ncomputers<\/strong>. The application continues to send SQL queries.\nInternally, the system determines which nodes hold the data and which\ncopies must participate in an operation.<\/p>\n<p>A typical design has several layers:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>SQL layer:<\/strong> receives queries, analyses them, plans\njoins and produces the execution steps.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Distribution and routing layer:<\/strong> assigns records to\nranges, shards or so-called tablets and finds their current storage\nlocations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Replication layer:<\/strong> manages multiple copies of data\nranges to enable continued operation during failures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Transaction coordination:<\/strong> where appropriate,\nensures that changes across multiple data ranges are correctly committed\nor discarded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Storage layer:<\/strong> keeps data on storage devices,\nmanages local indexes and performs recovery steps.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-a-distributed-booking\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture: A\ndistributed booking<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>   Booking request: ‘Reserve seat 42 for customer 7’\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                     SQL access node\n                         │\n                    Plan transaction\n                         │\n            ┌────────────┴────────────┐\n            ▼                         ▼\n    Data range ‘Seats’       Data range ‘Bookings’\n       Node A (+ copies)        Node B (+ copies)\n            │                         │\n            └────────────┬────────────┘\n                         ▼\n                    Coordinated commit\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                    Success or abort<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Importantly, the diagram shows <strong>one possible logical\narchitecture<\/strong>, not the precise internal process of every\nproduct. Some databases organise the layers differently, and not every\nSQL operation needs a transaction across multiple nodes.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"41-konsensverfahren-und-mehrheitsentscheidungen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Consensus\nalgorithms and majority decisions<\/h3>\n<p>When several copies of a data range exist, writes often need to be\nconfirmed in a common order. Some systems use a <strong>consensus\nalgorithm<\/strong> such as <em>Raft<\/em> for this. In simplified terms,\nin a group of three replicas, a majority of two decides which log\nentries are committed. If one node fails, the two remaining nodes can\noften still form a majority. If a network failure separates the nodes\ninto groups of one and two computers, the single-node group normally\ncannot perform new writes requiring majority confirmation.<\/p>\n<p>It must not be inferred from this that a Raft protocol alone solves\nevery database transaction. It orders changes within a particular\nreplication group. If a transaction affects several data ranges, further\ncoordination is required. The complete guarantees result from the\ninteraction of the storage, replication and transaction protocols.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> When a service genuinely needs\nmultiple nodes for increasing data volumes or write load, while still\nrequiring relational queries and strict correctness rules. <strong>When\nis it unnecessary?<\/strong> When a single server can easily handle the\nmeasured load. Additional distribution often comes at the cost of more\nnetwork traffic, higher latency and more complicated operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What distributed SQL does not automatically guarantee<\/h2>\n<p>An SQL interface is not a certification of identical features. Even\nwith PostgreSQL-like syntax, available data types, extensions,\ntransaction details, query plans, system catalogues and administrative\nfunctions may differ. An existing PostgreSQL project is therefore not\nguaranteed to be transferable without changes simply because the network\nprotocol is compatible.<\/p>\n<p>The expression <strong>‘horizontal scaling’<\/strong> also deserves\nscrutiny. More nodes can provide additional storage capacity and\nparallel processing. A single global query, a transaction across many\nnodes or a highly contended record does not become arbitrarily fast as a\nresult. Particularly difficult cases include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">global sorting and analytics;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">frequent joins across distribution boundaries;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">unique identifiers and other integrity rules across nodes;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">simultaneous changes to the same information;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">redistribution during live operation;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">access across large geographical distances.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For applications with few users, the additional network often brings\nno benefit. For large services, distribution may be necessary even\nthough individual accesses then become technically more demanding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>PostgreSQL: A powerful single system, replication and\nexternal extensions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong> is a specific, freely available\nrelational database system. It offers many of the features we have\nalready examined: indexes, transactions, various isolation levels,\ndeclarative table partitioning, physical replication and logical\nreplication.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Physical replication<\/strong> transfers, in particular,\nchanges from the <em>Write-Ahead Log<\/em> (WAL, a log of changes written\nin advance) to standby systems. In the appropriate mode, these can also\nexecute read queries. This extends availability or read capacity; in the\nusual primary–standby model, however, several independent primary\nservers for the same data are not transparently combined into a\ndistributed write system.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Logical replication<\/strong> transfers selected data changes\nusing logical data objects. It follows the publisher–subscriber\nprinciple: a publishing server makes changes available, and a\nsubscribing server applies them. This helps, for example, with selective\ndata transfer, migration or secondary analytical datasets. It is not\nautomatically conflict-free multi-primary writing; write conflicts must\nbe handled separately in more complex topologies.<\/p>\n<p>Built-in <strong>table partitioning<\/strong> can divide a large table\ninto internal sections. It does not replace automatic sharding across\nindependent servers. Additional software exists for this, for example\nthe PostgreSQL extension <strong>Citus<\/strong>, which provides\ncoordinator/worker and sharding features. The specific support, version\nand operating model of such a solution must be examined\nindependently.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-postgresql-with-a-read-replica\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nPostgreSQL with a read replica<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>        PHP application\n              │\n       ┌──────┴─────────┐\n       ▼                ▼\n     Changes         Read query\n       │                │\n       ▼                ▼\n   PostgreSQL        Standby /\n   primary server    read replica\n       │                ▲\n       └── WAL data ────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Such a system can cover a very broad range of applications and remain\neasier to operate than genuine sharding. Replication lag, switching\nduring failures and independent backups nevertheless remain important\ntasks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Crucial distinction:<\/strong> PostgreSQL itself, the\nPostgreSQL extension Citus and a separate distributed SQL product with\nPostgreSQL compatibility are <strong>three different\nthings<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Oracle: Single instance, RAC and globally distributed\ndatabase<\/h2>\n<p>When referring to ‘Oracle’, one must distinguish between the vendor,\nits traditional database product and an additional cluster architecture.\nIn 2026, the product documentation uses, among other names,\n<strong>Oracle AI Database 26ai<\/strong>. The technical differences, not\nthe marketing label, are what matter.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"71-oracle-database-als-einzelnes-datenbanksystem\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Oracle\nDatabase as a single database system<\/h3>\n<p>A normal Oracle database can operate as a single instance on one\nserver. In this basic model, an instance runs database processes and\naccesses the associated database files. In very simplified terms, this\nis comparable to the role of a single PostgreSQL instance, even though\nthe products, capabilities and licensing models differ.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"72-oracle-real-application-clusters-rac\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Oracle Real Application\nClusters (RAC)<\/h3>\n<p><strong>RAC<\/strong> stands for <em>Real Application Clusters<\/em>.\nThis Oracle technology allows several database instances on different\ncomputers to access <strong>the same logically shared database with\nshared storage<\/strong> at the same time. This requires clusterware,\ncoordinated shared storage, fast communication between nodes and\ncoordination of buffer contents.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-rac-with-a-shared-dataset\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nRAC with a shared dataset<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>              Application\n                  │\n             Database service\n                  │\n         ┌────────┴────────┐\n         ▼                 ▼\n    Oracle instance A  Oracle instance B\n       Computer 1         Computer 2\n         │                 │\n         └────────┬────────┘\n                  ▼\n           Shared, cluster-\n            capable storage\n            (the same files)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Several instances share <strong>one common logical dataset<\/strong>\nhere. They do not simply maintain their buffers independently without\ncoordination; the system organises buffer coherence. RAC can be useful\nfor availability and certain load profiles but is operationally\ndemanding. Shared storage and cluster coordination are central\narchitectural features.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RAC is not merely conventional read replication, but it is\nnot equivalent to independent sharding either.<\/strong> The data is not\nsimply held as separate subsets on each server.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"73-oracle-globally-distributed-ai-database-sharding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Oracle\nGlobally Distributed AI Database / sharding<\/h3>\n<p>The architecture officially named <strong>Oracle Globally Distributed\nAI Database<\/strong> distributes a <strong>logically related database\nacross independent physical Oracle databases<\/strong>, called shards.\nEach shard holds part of the data and does not need shared storage with\nother shards. A <em>shard director<\/em> can route connections to the\nappropriate shard according to the distribution key.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                Application\n                    │\n               Shard Director\n                    │\n         ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n         ▼          ▼          ▼\n     Oracle DB A  Oracle DB B  Oracle DB C\n     Customers    Customers    Customers\n        1–?          ...          ...\n     Own data     Own data     Own data\n     Own storage  Own storage  Own storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The division of customers is purely an illustrative example: actual\ndistribution may use different keys and rules. With sharding, data and\nload capacity can extend across several independent units. But questions\nalso arise about distributed queries, global transactions, placement and\nmanagement.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Direct comparison:<\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variant<\/th>\n<th>Basic idea<\/th>\n<th>Essential difference<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Single Oracle instance<\/td>\n<td>One database system with one instance<\/td>\n<td>No automatic multi-node distribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle RAC<\/td>\n<td>Several instances access the same dataset<\/td>\n<td>Shared, cluster-capable storage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle Globally Distributed AI Database<\/td>\n<td>Distribute data across independent shards<\/td>\n<td>Each shard has its own data and storage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For a small web project, neither RAC nor a globally distributed\nOracle architecture would be an obvious starting solution without a\nspecific operational requirement. Nor are these systems adequately\ndescribed merely by saying that ‘Oracle supports clusters’.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further real examples: MongoDB, CockroachDB and\nYugabyteDB<\/h2>\n<h3\nid=\"81-mongodb-dokumentendatenbank-mit-replikation-und-sharding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>MongoDB\n– Document database with replication and sharding<\/h3>\n<p>MongoDB is a document database. It can use both <strong>replica\nsets<\/strong> for replication and fault tolerance and <strong>sharded\nclusters<\/strong> for dividing a collection. In the cluster architecture\ndescribed, each shard is itself implemented as a replica set. A\n<code>mongos<\/code> router receives queries and determines their\ndestinations using metadata; configuration servers manage the cluster\ninformation.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>   Application\n       │\n       ▼\n    mongos router\n       │\n  ┌────┴──────────┐\n  ▼               ▼\nShard A         Shard B\n(Replica set)   (Replica set)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>shard key<\/strong> influences whether queries can reach a\nspecific node or have to be distributed across many shards. Depending on\n<code>readConcern<\/code>, <code>writeConcern<\/code>, session mode and\nread preference, MongoDB offers different consistency and durability\nguarantees. The blanket judgement ‘document databases are always only\neventually consistent’ would therefore be incorrect.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"82-cockroachdb-sql-ueber-verteiltem-schluessel-wert-speicher\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CockroachDB\n– SQL over distributed key-value storage<\/h3>\n<p>CockroachDB is an example of distributed SQL. The SQL layer provides\nrelational functions; underneath, the system distributes data in key\nranges (<em>ranges<\/em>) across different nodes. Replication groups use\nRaft. The product supports distributed transactions and, by default, the\n<strong>Serializable<\/strong> isolation level, so that from the\ndatabase’s perspective, concurrently executed transactions should\ncorrespond to a permissible serial execution.<\/p>\n<p>This is not a promise that every transaction prone to conflicts\nsucceeds on the first attempt. Under contention, transactions may need\nto be retried or restarted by the application. CockroachDB also uses\nPostgreSQL-like interfaces but is not identical to the PostgreSQL\ndatabase server or all PostgreSQL extensions.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"83-yugabytedb-postgresql-nahe-sql-schicht-und-verteilte-tablets\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>YugabyteDB\n– PostgreSQL-like SQL layer and distributed tablets<\/h3>\n<p>YugabyteDB combines a PostgreSQL-oriented SQL interface called\n<strong>YSQL<\/strong> with a distributed storage layer. Data is divided\ninto <em>tablets<\/em>; according to the project documentation, these are\ntypically managed using Raft replication groups. Here too, a logical\ndatabase is constructed from several physical units.<\/p>\n<p>YSQL compatibility can make it easier for developers experienced with\nPostgreSQL to get started. However, it does not mean that all features,\nextensions and versions are entirely identical. Whether a specific\nexisting application works without changes must be checked\nexplicitly.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"84-produkte-nicht-nach-blossem-etikett-auswaehlen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Do not select\nproducts merely by their labels<\/h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System/family<\/th>\n<th>Data model and interface<\/th>\n<th>Distribution principle<\/th>\n<th>Important limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PostgreSQL (core system)<\/td>\n<td>Relational, SQL<\/td>\n<td>Replication and table partitioning<\/td>\n<td>No automatically transparent distributed SQL multi-node write\ncluster<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PostgreSQL + Citus<\/td>\n<td>Relational, SQL<\/td>\n<td>Additional sharding/coordinator features<\/td>\n<td>Extension with its own functional and operational scope<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle RAC<\/td>\n<td>Relational, Oracle SQL<\/td>\n<td>Multiple instances with shared storage<\/td>\n<td>Cluster coordination and shared storage architecture<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle Globally Distributed AI Database<\/td>\n<td>Relational, Oracle SQL<\/td>\n<td>Independent shards<\/td>\n<td>Distributed data and queries require additional coordination<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MongoDB<\/td>\n<td>Documents, its own query interface<\/td>\n<td>Shards and replica sets<\/td>\n<td>Key selection, query distribution and differentiated consistency\nsettings<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CockroachDB<\/td>\n<td>Distributed SQL, PostgreSQL-compatible interfaces<\/td>\n<td>Ranges, Raft replication, distributed transactions<\/td>\n<td>Network and transaction coordination, not complete PostgreSQL\nequivalence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YugabyteDB (YSQL)<\/td>\n<td>Distributed SQL, PostgreSQL-like interface<\/td>\n<td>Tablets, Raft replication<\/td>\n<td>Compatibility and distribution must be checked for each feature<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This table describes architectural principles, not a product\nevaluation or pricing recommendation. Product variants and supported\nfunctions may change; the sources at the end document the reviewed\nstate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does consistency actually mean?<\/h2>\n<p>The word <strong>consistency<\/strong> has several meanings in the\ndatabase world. Misunderstandings arise particularly in distributed\nsystems when these meanings are confused.<\/p>\n<p><strong>ACID consistency:<\/strong> in the abbreviation\n<strong>ACID<\/strong> (<em>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation,\nDurability<\/em>), ‘Consistency’ refers to compliance with defined data\nrules and integrity constraints before and after successful\ntransactions. For example, an order number defined as unique must not\noccur twice. This is <strong>not the same<\/strong> as the meaning of\nconsistency in the CAP theorem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Replica consistency:<\/strong> which version of data is\nvisible when the same data resides on several servers? Could a replica\nstill display an earlier balance while the primary server has already\nacknowledged a change?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Transaction isolation:<\/strong> what do concurrently executed\ntransactions observe of one another? A database may offer a particular\ntransaction isolation level while some replicas nevertheless return\ndelayed data. The two questions must be considered separately.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"91-starke-konsistenz-und-linearisierbarkeit\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Strong consistency\nand linearisability<\/h3>\n<p>In the distributed context, <strong>linearisability<\/strong> means,\nin simplified terms, that each individual operation appears to have\ntaken place at a particular point between its invocation and its\nresponse, respecting the actual order of non-overlapping operations. If\nan acknowledged change has completed in full and a corresponding new\nread operation starts afterwards, it should not simply return an older\nvalue.<\/p>\n<p>This is a strong property. It may be necessary for important\ninventory, reservation or lock information. However, it may introduce\nwaiting time and, during network problems, prevent an isolated subgroup\nfrom acknowledging writes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Serializable<\/strong> and <strong>linearizable<\/strong>, in\nturn, are not synonyms. Serializable primarily assesses whether the\nresult of concurrently executed transactions corresponds to a serial\nexecution; linearisability also considers the real-time ordering of\nindividual operations. The precise guarantees are product-specific.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"92-eventual-consistency-letztlich-uebereinstimmende-kopien\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Eventual\nconsistency – copies that ultimately converge<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Eventual consistency<\/strong> describes how, under suitable\nconditions, particularly when no further changes occur and communication\nresumes, copies converge towards the same value over time. Immediately\nafter a change, different nodes may show different states.<\/p>\n<p>For example, the counter ‘How many times has an image been viewed?’\nmay temporarily differ across a public gallery. For an available seat in\na paid booking, however, the same delay may be unacceptable.<\/p>\n<p>Eventual consistency therefore <strong>does not<\/strong> mean that a\nsystem can be arbitrarily incorrect or that no rules apply at all. The\napplication must decide which temporary differences it tolerates and how\nconflicts are reconciled.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"93-weitere-wichtige-garantien\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further important guarantees<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Read your writes:<\/strong> a user sees their own\nacknowledged changes in subsequent corresponding reads.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Monotonic reads:<\/strong> during successive reads, a user\ndoes not return to an older, already superseded state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Causal consistency:<\/strong> changes that causally depend on\none another are observed in an appropriate order, even if independent\nevents can be distributed more freely.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Quorum:<\/strong> sufficient replicas must agree to certain\ndecisions; the proportion required depends on the particular\nprotocol.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The specific combination depends on the database and its settings.\nDocument databases can offer strong operations; relational databases can\nreturn outdated values on delayed read replicas. The SQL/NoSQL label\nalone does not decide this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The CAP theorem – what it really says<\/h2>\n<p><strong>CAP<\/strong> stands for <strong>Consistency<\/strong>,\n<strong>Availability<\/strong> and <strong>Partition Tolerance<\/strong>.\nThe theorem is often simplified to ‘You can only have two out of three\nproperties’. This shorthand is misleading without further\nexplanation.<\/p>\n<p>In this context, the three concepts mean, in simplified terms:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>C – Consistency:<\/strong> in the usual CAP model, this\nrefers to a strong, linearised view of data access, not merely\ncompliance with table rules.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A – Availability:<\/strong> every request to a participating\nnode that has not failed should receive a valid response. A mere ‘Error:\ncurrently unavailable’ does not satisfy this strong availability\nrequirement as a successful operation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>P – Partition tolerance:<\/strong> the distributed system\nmust take into account that messages between parts of the network may be\nlost or may not arrive in time. A <strong>network partition<\/strong>\nprevents nodes from communicating with one another.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-when-the-network-splits\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nWhen the network splits<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>              Shared data state: stock = 1\n\n           Server A          Server B\n              │                │\n              └── Network ─────┘\n\n                  NETWORK PARTITION\n\n           Server A       X       Server B\n              │                      │\n       User 1: ‘buy’          User 2: ‘buy’\n              │                      │\n       Connection between servers disrupted<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If both sides independently sell the same last item and confirm both\npurchases, the shared stock state may become incorrect. If, by contrast,\none side waits for sufficiently confirmed coordination or rejects a\nrequest, the strict availability requirement that ‘every non-failed node\nresponds successfully’ is violated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The core of the theorem:<\/strong> under the assumptions\ninvestigated by Gilbert and Lynch, a distributed system cannot\nsimultaneously guarantee unrestricted linearisable consistency and this\nstrong form of availability for all affected operations during a network\npartition. It is a <em>conflict arising during communication\nfailures<\/em>, not an ordinary menu from which to select three\nproperties during normal operation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"101-wie-zwei-typische-reaktionen-aussehen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What two typical\nresponses look like<\/h3>\n<p>A system can favour strict consistency when nodes are separated: only\na sufficiently coordinated side confirms critical changes. The isolated\nother part may temporarily be unable to complete corresponding writes\nsuccessfully. Such an architecture is often informally called\n<strong>CP-oriented<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Alternatively, a system can continue allowing local responses and\nchanges on separated sides and require subsequent reconciliation. Strict\nconsistency may then be lost during the partition; this is often called\n<strong>AP-oriented<\/strong> in simplified terms. How conflicts are\ndetected and resolved is part of the specific application design.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> many databases offer different guarantees\ndepending on operation, settings and topology. It is often imprecise to\nlabel an entire product ‘the CP database’ or ‘the AP database’ without\nthese qualifications.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"102-was-cap-nicht-erklaert\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What CAP does not explain<\/h3>\n<p>CAP does not predict which database is fastest, how expensive it\nbecomes or which query language it supports. Nor does CAP state that a\nsystem must always disable one of the three properties when the network\nis functioning normally. The practically important trade-off between low\nlatency and stronger coordination exists even without a network\npartition; this additional trade-off is described, among other things,\nby <strong>PACELC<\/strong>. Selecting an actual product therefore\nrequires more than its CAP label.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The geographical factor: Multiple data centres do not\nchange the physics<\/h2>\n<p>Distributed data storage becomes particularly demanding when nodes\nare located in different cities or countries. Within a data centre,\ncommunication paths are usually relatively short. Between distant\nregions, signals and data packets take more time, and connections may\nfail independently of one another.<\/p>\n<p>If a write counts as acknowledged only after several geographically\ndistributed replicas agree, the application must wait for their\ncommunication. More nodes therefore do not automatically increase the\nspeed of an individual transaction. At the same time, geographical\ndistribution may help survive a larger regional failure, provided that\nplacement, replication and recovery have been planned appropriately.<\/p>\n<p>Legal questions can also play a role. The location of personal data,\nthe use of external processors, transfers to other countries and\ndeletion or retention obligations must be examined for the specific\nservice. The technical ability to replicate data across continents does\nnot automatically constitute permission under data protection law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical examples – the appropriate architecture for\neach<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"121-lokaler-medienkatalog\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local media catalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local program manages several hundred thousand entries relating to\nimages, videos and metadata. Much of the work consists of searching,\nsorting and occasional changes. <strong>A local SQLite database<\/strong>\ncan be very useful here. The size of the table alone is not a reason to\noperate a distributed database system. Good indexes, a comprehensible\ndata structure and reliable backups usually solve the more important\nproblems in this scenario.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"122-kleiner-bezahlter-webdienst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small paid web service<\/h3>\n<p>A PHP tool offers customers analytical services. It has accounts, job\ndata and results. As long as the load is manageable, <strong>one\napplication server and one PostgreSQL database<\/strong> make a\nreasonable starting architecture. If availability or read volume becomes\nmore important later, standby or read-replica replication may be useful.\nA complete distributed SQL cluster would initially be additional\ncomplexity without a measured bottleneck.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"123-buchungsdienst-mit-mehreren-regionen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Booking service across\nmultiple regions<\/h3>\n<p>A large service manages a limited number of reservation places for\nusers at different locations. The same place must not be sold twice with\nbinding confirmation. Strict correctness, unambiguous write authority,\ntransactional guarantees and defined responses to failures become more\nimportant here. A well-designed central database with failover may be\nsufficient; a distributed SQL architecture is <strong>one\npossible<\/strong>, but not necessarily the best, solution. What matters\nis which response is acceptable during failures: ‘booking currently\nunavailable’ may be better for the business process than an incorrectly\nconfirmed double booking.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>AI job queue<\/strong> has different requirements again:\nthe job database needs clear states and unique job identifiers, but\nexpensive image or model processing takes place on workers. Additional\nGPU computers do not automatically mean that the database also has to be\nsharded.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operations, risks and costs<\/h2>\n<p>A technically sophisticated database architecture rarely solves all\noperational problems by itself. At least the following questions must be\nexamined:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Why is it important?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who updates the nodes?<\/td>\n<td>Security vulnerabilities and version differences may jeopardise\noperation.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who monitors replication and quorum?<\/td>\n<td>An apparently functioning application may be unable to confirm new\nchanges safely.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How is data restored?<\/td>\n<td>Replication also propagates erroneous deletions; independent backups\nremain necessary.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How is a schema changed?<\/td>\n<td>Distributed environments may involve longer migrations and version\ndifferences.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where are data and copies located?<\/td>\n<td>Data protection, latency and regional failures are affected.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which transactions require multiple shards?<\/td>\n<td>Network coordination and contention can worsen response times.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How are credentials protected?<\/td>\n<td>Additional nodes and services increase the attack surface.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What does ongoing operation cost?<\/td>\n<td>Compute nodes, storage, replicas, data traffic, monitoring and\nsupport incur continuing costs.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A provider may take on some of this work as a <strong>managed\nservice<\/strong>. That does not remove responsibility for the functional\ndata model, permissions, backup objectives, abuse prevention and correct\nhandling of application errors. Managed services also incur running\ncosts and can create dependencies on particular functions or licensing\nmodels.<\/p>\n<p><strong>No cost ranking without measurements:<\/strong> a small server\nmay be cheaper and simpler than a multi-node cluster. With very high\nactual load, on the other hand, a cluster may become technically or\neconomically necessary. The decision requires real requirements,\nobserved bottlenecks and a comparison of the full operational costs, not\nmerely a marketing term.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision questions before a database migration<\/h2>\n<p>Before rebuilding an existing application around distributed\ndatabases, five questions should be answered:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What is the demonstrated bottleneck?<\/strong> Reading,\nwriting, storage, availability or geographical distance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Which data must never be inconsistent?<\/strong> For example,\ninventory, payments, reservations or access rights.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>What may temporarily be outdated?<\/strong> For example,\nstatistical counters, public search indexes or derived views.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Which interfaces does the new database actually need to\nsupport?<\/strong> SQL syntax, transactions, drivers, extensions, data\ntypes, migration tools.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Who operates, backs up and pays for the system?<\/strong>\nMonitoring, updates, recovery, fault diagnosis, data protection and user\nsupport.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>If an existing, well-maintained single database already satisfies all\nrequirements, a migration is above all one thing: <strong>an unnecessary\nrisk<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook for M3-L5<\/h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we have distinguished several levels:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>SQL<\/strong> is a query language;\n<strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong> and <strong>Oracle Database<\/strong> are\nspecific relational database systems.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NoSQL<\/strong> is an umbrella family of different data\nmodels, not a uniform statement about consistency.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>PostgreSQL replication<\/strong> copies data;\n<strong>PostgreSQL table partitioning<\/strong> divides tables\ninternally; additional sharding solutions must be distinguished from\nthese.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Oracle RAC<\/strong> operates with several instances on\nshared storage; <strong>Oracle Globally Distributed AI Database<\/strong>\ndistributes data across independent shards.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Distributed SQL<\/strong> combines SQL and relational\nfunctions with internally coordinated data distribution, but not without\nnetwork, transaction and operational effort.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>CAP<\/strong> describes a fundamental trade-off during\nnetwork partitions, not a general ranking of all databases or a simple,\nfreely chosen ‘two out of three’ rule.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>We now know the basic architectural variants. In <strong><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Transactions and distributed\ncorrectness<\/a><\/strong>, we next examine more closely how to correctly\nprocess concurrent changes, retries, duplicate messages and multi-stage\ntransactions. Particularly in reservations, payments and AI job chains,\nthis level determines whether an application genuinely works\nreliably.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>All product features mentioned refer to the <strong>source status as\nof 03.11.2026<\/strong>. The official documentation may change later. The\nfollowing links are primary sources; the key statements in this lesson\nwere checked against them:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>High Availability, Load Balancing, and\nReplication<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/high-availability.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/high-availability.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Logical Replication<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Table Partitioning<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Oracle Database 26ai, <em>Overview of Oracle RAC Architecture and\nProcessing<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/racad/introduction-to-oracle-rac/overview-of-oracle-rac-architecture-and-processing.html\">https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/racad/introduction-to-oracle-rac/overview-of-oracle-rac-architecture-and-processing.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Oracle Database 26ai, <em>Oracle Globally Distributed AI Database –\nArchitecture and Components<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/shard/architecture-components.html\">https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/shard/architecture-components.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Oracle Database 26ai, <em>Oracle Globally Distributed AI Database\nOverview<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/shard/overview1.html\">https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/shard/overview1.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">MongoDB, <em>Sharding<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sharding/\">https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sharding/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">MongoDB, <em>Read Isolation, Consistency, and Recency<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/read-isolation-consistency-recency/\">https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/read-isolation-consistency-recency/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">9.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">CockroachDB, <em>Developer Basics<\/em> (SQL compatibility, isolation\nlevels, retries): <a\nhref=\"https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/developer-basics.html\">https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/developer-basics.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">10.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">YugabyteDB, <em>Architecture<\/em> and <em>DocDB Replication<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/architecture/\">https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/architecture/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/architecture/docdb-replication/\">https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/architecture/docdb-replication/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">11.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gilbert, Seth / Lynch, Nancy (2002), <em>Brewer’s conjecture and the\nfeasibility of consistent, available, partition-tolerant web\nservices<\/em>, ACM SIGACT News 33(2): <a\nhref=\"https://apice.unibo.it/bin/view/Publication/CapproofSigactnews33\">https://apice.unibo.it/bin/view/Publication/CapproofSigactnews33<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Reading note:<\/strong> technical properties differ by product\nversion, configuration, operating environment and specific operation.\nThe simplified examples (booking numbers, customer distribution, node\ncounts) are not claims about the performance limits of any particular\nvendor.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Transactions and distributed correctness<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / bridge to Master’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: A successful request does not\nautomatically mean a correct change<\/h2>\n<p>A database with only one user is easy to imagine: an application\nreads a value, changes it and writes it back. As soon as several users,\nbackground processes or servers act simultaneously, this simple model is\nno longer sufficient. One part of the system might read data that has\nchanged in the meantime. A message might arrive twice. Or server A\nconfirms a change while server B knows nothing about it yet.<\/p>\n<p>The fundamental problem is <strong>correctness under concurrent and\ndistributed changes<\/strong>. An application should not merely respond\nin some way: its business rules must remain valid even when operations\noverlap and individual components fail.<\/p>\n<p>An easily understood example is seat reservation. Two requests for\nthe last available seat arrive simultaneously. Both initially read “one\nseat available”. If both then independently store “reserved”, we have\ntwo confirmations for a single seat. Every individual program\ninstruction may have been technically correct; the <em>overall\neffect<\/em> would be wrong.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request A ── reads “1 seat free” ─┐\n                                 ├── both want to reserve\nRequest B ── reads “1 seat free” ─┘\n                                 ↓\n                      correctness decision needed\n                                 ↓\n                      at most one valid confirmation<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Which technique solves this problem depends on whether both requests\nuse the same database or the data resides on independent servers. We\nbegin with the simpler case and gradually increase the complexity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What a database transaction guarantees<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>transaction<\/strong> is a logically related sequence of\ndatabase operations treated under certain guarantees. It often begins,\nperforms several changes and receives final confirmation only at the end\n(<em>commit<\/em>). If it fails before then, the database can undo the\nchanges that have not yet been committed (<em>rollback<\/em>).<\/p>\n<p>A transaction can be understood as a complete business operation.\nWhen 20 euros are transferred from one account to another, “debit 20\neuros” and “credit 20 euros” belong together. The first change without\nthe second would be problematic from a business perspective.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Transaction “transfer 20 euros”\n       │\n       ├── Account A: -20 euros\n       ├── Account B: +20 euros\n       │\n       └── COMMIT: both changes take effect\n            or\n           ROLLBACK: both are discarded<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The familiar properties of such processing are summarised as\n<strong>ACID<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Abbreviation<\/th>\n<th>Full term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in everyday language<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td><em>Atomicity<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Either the entire transaction or none of its changes takes\neffect.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td><em>Consistency<\/em><\/td>\n<td>The database preserves its defined integrity rules; additional\nbusiness rules must be modelled correctly.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I<\/td>\n<td><em>Isolation<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Concurrent transactions should not affect one another in\nimpermissible ways.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<td><em>Durability<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Committed changes should survive the guaranteed failure scenarios,\nsubject to configuration and the failure model.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> ACID consistency does not mean that the\ndatabase itself knows every business rule. If the system defines no\nprotection against duplicate ticket reservations, it may atomically\nstore a state that is wrong from a business perspective. Business rules\nmust be implemented as constraints, appropriate changes or verifiable\ntransaction logic.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> Displaying a static\ninformation page does not require a write transaction. By contrast, it\nis often central to related changes involving monetary amounts, orders\nor permissions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why concurrent access can cause errors<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a counter with the value 10. Two processes each want to add\n1.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Initial value: 10\n\nProcess A: reads 10       Process B: reads 10\nProcess A: calculates 11  Process B: calculates 11\nProcess A: writes 11      Process B: writes 11\n\nFinal value: 11           Expected: 12<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is a classic example of a <strong>lost update<\/strong>. The\nsecond write overwrites the result of the first. Depending on the\ndatabase system, isolation level and form of the update, the database\ncan prevent this or report an error. The decisive factor is whether the\napplication first reads the value outside the database and then writes\nit back without protection, or uses, for example, an appropriate atomic\nupdate and correct conflict handling.<\/p>\n<p>Another difficulty is a <strong>non-repeatable read<\/strong>: one\noperation reads a row, another changes it and commits the change, and\nthe first reads a different value in a later execution of the same\nquery. With <strong>phantom reads<\/strong>, instead of a particular row\nchanging, the set of results changes because matching records have been\nadded or removed in the meantime.<\/p>\n<p>With <strong>write skew<\/strong>, even transactions that modify\ndifferent rows can jointly violate a business rule. Example: two doctors\nmust ensure that at least one is always on call. Both read “the other\nperson is staying on duty” and independently remove themselves from the\nschedule. If the isolation used does not sufficiently prevent this\nconflict, nobody is on duty afterwards. The rows do not conflict\ndirectly; the <em>jointly evaluated rule<\/em> does.<\/p>\n<p>The lesson is that it is not sufficient to store each individual row\ncorrectly. Relationships between several rows or conditions spanning\nentire data sets often need to be preserved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Isolation: How strongly should transactions be\nseparated?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Isolation<\/strong> describes which effects of parallel\ntransactions an operation may see. SQL defines several classical\nisolation levels. Their implementation is database-specific; the\nfollowing terms initially serve as orientation.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Isolation level<\/th>\n<th>Guiding idea<\/th>\n<th>Typical trade-off<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Read Uncommitted<\/td>\n<td>Weakest classical level; reading uncommitted changes is possible in\nprinciple<\/td>\n<td>Risk of unreliable intermediate values; PostgreSQL treats this level\nas Read Committed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read Committed<\/td>\n<td>Changes read are committed; new commits may become visible between\ntwo queries<\/td>\n<td>Good for many standard operations, but not every multi-step\nrule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeatable Read<\/td>\n<td>A more consistent view throughout the transaction<\/td>\n<td>Product-specific handling of conflicts and anomalies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serializable<\/td>\n<td>The result corresponds to a possible serial ordering of complete\ntransactions<\/td>\n<td>Additional checks, conflict-related aborts and retries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Serialisability<\/strong> does not necessarily mean that\neverything actually runs sequentially. The system may process operations\nin parallel as long as the final result is as if a permitted order had\noccurred.<\/p>\n<p>PostgreSQL 18 uses <strong>Multi-Version Concurrency Control\n(MVCC)<\/strong>: under appropriate conditions, readers and writers can\nsee different record versions instead of blocking one another on every\noperation. At <em>Serializable<\/em>, PostgreSQL can abort transactions\nif an impermissible overlap is imminent. Applications must then be\nprepared for a clean retry.<\/p>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL-specific clarification:<\/strong> In PostgreSQL,\n<code>Repeatable Read<\/code> also prevents classical phantom reads, but\nstill allows certain <strong>serialisation anomalies<\/strong>. The\n<em>write skew<\/em> described above can be particularly relevant to a\nbusiness rule spanning several rows. <code>Serializable<\/code> performs\nadditional checks and may in this case reject one of the affected\ntransactions with a serialisation failure. The application may then need\nto restart the <strong>entire transaction<\/strong> using current data. A\nhigh isolation level replaces neither correct integrity rules nor\nsensible access patterns. Reference: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html\">PostgreSQL\n18 – Transaction Isolation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_021_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Transaction with BEGIN and COMMIT or ROLLBACK; MVCC example with price versions of 10 and 12 euros read by different transactions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Transaction with BEGIN and COMMIT or\nROLLBACK; MVCC example with price versions of 10 and 12 euros read by\ndifferent transactions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simplified <strong>PostgreSQL Repeatable Read<\/strong>\nexample: T1 already has an older snapshot and continues to see version\nv1 (€10) after T2 commits the change to v2 (€12). A subsequently started\nT3 sees v2. Snapshot timing and isolation are decisive; MVCC alone does\n<strong>not<\/strong> guarantee serialisability, and\n<code>Serializable<\/code> can require retries following conflict-related\naborts.<\/em><\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Two transactions at once\n             │\n             ▼\n     Database tracks versions\n     and possible conflicts\n             │\n       ┌─────┴─────┐\n       ▼           ▼\n   Safe sequence   Conflict detected\n       │           │\n     Commit        Abort\n                   │\n                   ▼\n          Retry entire transaction if needed<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Practical implication:<\/strong> The strictest isolation is\nnot required for every query. It may be appropriate for critical rules,\nbut depending on load it leads to additional conflict-handling work and\naborts. The appropriate level should derive from the business risk, not\nfrom a desire for the strongest-sounding guarantees.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Locks, optimistic conflict checks and deadlocks<\/h2>\n<p>Databases use various mechanisms to coordinate concurrent changes.\n<strong>Locks<\/strong> are one of them: a transaction can reserve a row\nor other resource so that conflicting operations must wait.<\/p>\n<p>With a <strong>pessimistic strategy<\/strong>, appropriate locks\nprevent the conflict early. With an <strong>optimistic\nstrategy<\/strong>, operations may initially proceed and check on\ncompletion whether someone has changed the same relevant state in the\nmeantime. A version field or the database system’s conflict detection\ncan help.<\/p>\n<p>Neither strategy is universally better. Locks can cause waiting and\nblocking; optimistic methods can cause frequent retries under high\ncontention.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>deadlock<\/strong> arises when several transactions wait for\none another:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Transaction A locks record X\nTransaction B locks record Y\n\nA now wants Y ───────► waits for B\nB now wants X ───────► waits for A\n\n                  Standstill\n                    │\n          Database detects deadlock\n                    │\n           One transaction is aborted<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>PostgreSQL detects such deadlocks and aborts one of the participating\ntransactions. Applications should then not blindly re-execute every type\nof failed operation, but retry the entire operation where appropriate.\nThe risk can often be reduced by accessing multiple resources in a\nconsistent order and keeping transactions short.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong> A private single-user\ncatalogue with few changes does not normally require complex custom lock\nmanagement. The transaction guarantees of the database used may be\nsufficient. With concurrent reservations, stock levels or payment\nassignments, however, the importance increases substantially.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why “try again” can be dangerous in a distributed\nsystem<\/h2>\n<p>A network call has at least three possible outcomes: the destination\nprocesses the request successfully, rejects it, or the connection fails\nalong the way. In the third case, the caller may not know <em>whether\nthe change has already occurred<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose a customer clicks “Order”. The application creates the order.\nThe connection then fails before the confirmation reaches the browser.\nThe browser retries the request. Without protection, a second order can\nbe created.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ── “create order 4711” ─────► Server\n   ▲                                   │\n   │                 Database stores   │\n   │                                   ▼\n   └──── Response lost in network    [stored]\n\nBrowser ── Same request again ──────► Server\n                       Risk: second order<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A repeated attempt is called a <strong>retry<\/strong>. Retries are\nuseful during temporary failures, but do not themselves resolve\nuncertainty. A timeout proves neither that the original operation failed\nnor that it succeeded.<\/p>\n<p>Important write operations therefore often require an\n<strong>idempotency strategy<\/strong>. <strong>Idempotent<\/strong> means\nthat performing the same intended operation several times has the same\nbusiness-level final effect as performing it once.<\/p>\n<p>Example: each order receives a unique <strong>operation\nidentifier<\/strong> reliably established by the caller or application.\nThe server stores this identifier with the result under a uniqueness\nconstraint. If the same identifier arrives again, the existing operation\nis recognised instead of creating a second order.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request + operation identifier K123\n               │\n               ▼\n      Identifier already stored?\n           /             \\\n          No             Yes\n          │               │\n     Commit operation   Associate previous\n     once               result with K123\n                        (no duplicate effect)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For a robust implementation, the identifier and result must be stored\nconsistently. The system also needs rules concerning validity periods,\nuse of the same identifier with different payloads and concurrency when\ntwo identical requests arrive simultaneously. A volatile list in working\nmemory is often insufficient across restarts.<\/p>\n<p>In the <strong>Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)<\/strong>, the\nmethods <code>GET<\/code>, <code>PUT<\/code> and <code>DELETE<\/code> are\nregarded as safe or idempotent in different respects according to their\ndefined semantics; <code>POST<\/code> is not generally idempotent. The\nactual business implementation remains decisive, not merely the method\nname. <code>GET<\/code> should not cause a business-level change; a\ncarelessly implemented <code>GET /zahlung-ausloesen<\/code> contradicts\nprecisely this idea.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Duplicate messages are more normal than one might think<\/h2>\n<p>Online services do not communicate only directly via HTTP. They often\nsend events or jobs to a <strong>message broker<\/strong>, which buffers\nthem and delivers them to <strong>workers<\/strong> (background\nprocesses). A worker may crash after processing a message but before\nacknowledging its successful receipt. The broker may then deliver the\nmessage again.<\/p>\n<p><strong>At-least-once delivery<\/strong> means that under the\nrespective operating assumptions at least one delivery is sought;\nduplicates are possible. <strong>At-most-once delivery<\/strong> tends to\navoid duplicates under the respective semantics at the cost of possible\nlosses. Comprehensive <em>exactly-once<\/em> behaviour across message\ndelivery, the database and external side effects is not guaranteed\nsimply by enabling a broker option.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Job queue\n        │\n        ▼\n     Worker A ── Processes job ──────► Database\n        │\n        └── Crash before acknowledgement\n                    │\n           Broker delivers again\n                    │\n                    ▼\n                 Worker B\n                    │\n          Checks processing identifier\n          and avoids duplicate effect<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A common method is the <strong>inbox or processing table<\/strong>:\nthe database stores the unique message identifier together with the\nbusiness-level change in one transaction. When a job arrives again, the\nsystem recognises that its effect has already been applied.<\/p>\n<p>Furthermore, messages do not automatically travel in the order in\nwhich they were generated. With multiple workers, responses and changes\ncan arrive in a different order. If order matters, we need suitable\nkeys, sequence numbers, versions or processing ordered per\ncustomer/object.<\/p>\n<p>For a local batch process with only one controlled execution path,\nthis infrastructure may be unnecessary. However, once servers, queues\nand external APIs are involved, duplicate processing becomes a\nfundamental issue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Updating two systems: The dual-write problem<\/h2>\n<p>An online service stores an order in its database and then wants to\nsend an “order created” message. A naïve procedure consists of two\nindependent steps:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1. Database: store order                        ✓\n2. Message broker: publish event               ✗ Failure\n\nResult: the order exists,\n        other services were not informed.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Reversing the order does not reliably help: if the message is sent\nfirst and the database access subsequently fails, a notification may\nexist for an operation that was never stored. This fundamental problem\nis called <strong>dual write<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"transactional-outbox\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transactional outbox<\/h3>\n<p>A common countermeasure is the <strong>transactional outbox<\/strong>.\nThe application writes the actual change and a dispatch request\n<strong>within the same local database transaction<\/strong>. A separate\nprocess sends the outbox entry later.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Web application\n     │\n     ▼\nOne local database transaction\n     ├── Store order\n     └── Store outbox event\n     │\n     ▼\n   COMMIT\n     │\n     ▼\nOutbox worker ──► Message broker ──► Recipient\n                             │\n                  On retry: detect duplicates<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This closes the critical gap between a <em>local change<\/em> and a\n<em>durably recorded message awaiting dispatch<\/em>. However, it does\nnot itself guarantee exactly-once processing at the recipient. An\nacknowledgement can be lost during dispatch; the outbox process may then\nsend again. <strong>An outbox and an idempotent recipient therefore\nbelong together.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The technical effort is not free: dispatch status, pending entries,\nfailures, backlogs and clean-up must be monitored. For a single program\nwithout external message dispatch, the pattern is not normally\nworthwhile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distributed transactions: When several databases must decide\ntogether<\/h2>\n<p>Now we distribute an operation across <strong>two independent\ndatabases<\/strong>. Server A should debit an amount of money and server\nB should credit it. Starting an ordinary ACID transaction on each is not\nsufficient: initially, the two transactions know nothing of each\nother.<\/p>\n<p>A higher-level method is needed for the joint decision. A classical\napproach is <strong>Two-Phase Commit (2PC)<\/strong>. A\n<strong>coordinator<\/strong> consults several participants.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Preparation phase:<\/strong> The coordinator asks whether\nevery participant can durably prepare and later commit its planned\nchanges. The participants agree or reject.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Decision phase:<\/strong> If all agree, the coordinator\ndecides to commit; otherwise, to roll back. This decision must reliably\nreach all participants.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>              Transaction coordinator\n                      │\n        ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n        ▼                           ▼\n     Database A                  Database B\n      “ready?”                    “ready?”\n        │                           │\n      Yes/No                      Yes/No\n        └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n                      ▼\n                  Joint decision\n                      │\n        ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n        ▼                           ▼\n   Commit/Rollback             Commit/Rollback<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Why is 2PC not the default everywhere?<\/strong> During\npreparation, resources may remain reserved. If the coordinator fails at\na critical moment, participants may have to wait for recovery of the\ndecision. The method requires support from all participating systems and\nadditional operational logic.<\/p>\n<p>PostgreSQL 18 supports prepared transactions for an external\ntransaction manager. This does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that two\narbitrary PostgreSQL servers automatically become a correctly\ncoordinated transaction system through a simple switch. The coordinator\nand the failure/recovery procedure remain essential components.<\/p>\n<p>Modern distributed SQL databases also coordinate transactions, but\nusing product-specific mechanisms. It would be wrong to assume the same\n2PC implementation everywhere.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit of applicability:<\/strong> If a small application\nrequires only one database, a local transaction is usually much simpler,\neasier to understand and less expensive.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sagas and compensation: Business processes instead of one\nlarge lock<\/h2>\n<p>Not every long-running operation can or should occur within one\nglobal transaction. An order can reserve goods, initiate payment\nprocessing, create a shipping request and later trigger an email. Such\nsteps often involve different services or external partners.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>saga<\/strong> divides a related business process into\nseveral local transactions. After every successful step, the next one\nstarts. If a later step fails permanently, suitable\n<strong>compensations<\/strong> (<em>compensating actions<\/em>) follow,\nsuch as “release the reservation again”.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1. Create order        ──► successful\n2. Reserve items       ──► successful\n3. Authorise payment   ──► failed\n                             │\n                             ▼\n                    Release reservation\n                    Set order to “cancelled”<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Compensation is not the same as database rollback.<\/strong> A\ncompleted local transaction has already been committed and may have been\nseen by other operations in the meantime. The subsequent counteraction\nis a new business operation. A shipped parcel cannot simply be “rolled\nback”; a return may need to be arranged. Payments, notifications and\nexternal side effects also have their own rules.<\/p>\n<p>Sagas can be <strong>orchestrated<\/strong>: a central process manager\ndecides which step to trigger next. Or they can be\n<strong>choreographed<\/strong>: services react to events from other\nservices. Both models must handle partial failures, retries, missing\nresponses and uncertain intermediate states.<\/p>\n<p>A saga is particularly suitable when a business operation runs over a\nlonger period and across several independent systems and temporary\nintermediate states are acceptable. Where an invalid intermediate state\nmust never become visible, a local or suitable strict distributed\ntransaction may instead be required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Eventual consistency: Why temporarily differing views can\nbe acceptable<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Eventual consistency<\/strong> roughly means that, if no new\nchanges are added and the necessary communication functions again, the\nrelevant copies converge towards a consistent state according to the\nsystem’s guarantees. It does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that every\narbitrary delayed change eventually becomes correct at the business\nlevel automatically.<\/p>\n<p>An image portal might store a new image description immediately in\nthe application’s primary store and update the public search function\nonly later. For a few seconds, search then shows an older state. This is\nacceptable to many users.<\/p>\n<p>In an ordering system, by contrast, an outdated account balance or a\nreservation allocated twice can cause considerable damage. Here one must\ndistinguish which information may appear with a delay and which\ndecisions must be made immediately on an unambiguous, current basis.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Image portal:                   Booking system:\nUpload image                    Reserve last seat\n    │                                 │\n    ▼                                 ▼\nDatabase commits                Strict availability check\n    │                                 │\n    ├── Search index later            ▼\n    └── Preview image later     Unambiguous reservation\n\nDelay usually tolerable         Duplicate confirmation intolerable<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An application can use <strong>both strategies at once<\/strong>: a\nstrictly correct booking decision, but delayed email confirmation or\nsearch index updates. This separation reduces complexity and avoids\nunnecessarily rigid overall architectures.<\/p>\n<p>Important consistency questions include: Does the user see their own\ncommitted change (<em>read your writes<\/em>)? Are dependent events\nprocessed in the correct order? Can a second server return older data?\nAnd what should happen if a location is currently unreachable?<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_022_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Primary database commits a change while a delayed read replica may still display an old value; three strategies for read your writes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Primary database commits a change while a\ndelayed read replica may still display an old value; three strategies\nfor read your writes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: A deliberately simplified example of asynchronous\nreplication and <strong>eventual consistency<\/strong>. A committed write\nmay not yet be visible in a subsequent query through a delayed replica\nserver. <strong>Read your writes<\/strong> therefore requires a suitable\nread source or version coordination; this is distinct from the\ntransaction isolation levels explained earlier.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous example: Three servers, two orders, one\nremaining unit<\/h2>\n<p>Consider an online tool that sells a limited number of processing\ncredits. A customer account has <strong>one credit<\/strong> remaining.\nTwo browser windows simultaneously send an AI image job that costs one\ncredit each.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"variant-a-uncoordinated-processing\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variant A: Uncoordinated\nprocessing<\/h3>\n<p>Server 1 and server 2 both read the credit balance of 1. Both start\ntheir AI worker and later each deduct one credit. The result might be a\nnegative balance, a lost update or two paid services for the price of\none. The decisive point is that reading and the subsequent decision were\nnot sufficiently coupled.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"variant-b-one-database-as-the-authoritative-decision-point\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variant\nB: One database as the authoritative decision point<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser A ──► Web server 1 ─┐\n                             ├──► Same database\nBrowser B ──► Web server 2 ─┘          │\n                                       ▼\n                           Reserve credit atomically\n                           only if balance sufficient\n                                       │\n                            ┌──────────┴─────────┐\n                            ▼                    ▼\n                          Success             Rejection\n                            │\n                      Job in queue\n                            │\n                            ▼\n                         AI worker<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The important idea is that all web servers may run in parallel while\n<strong>one consistent database operation<\/strong> authoritatively\ndecides which job is valid. A suitable unique job identifier\nadditionally protects against accidental duplicate submission. The job\nis accepted only after successful reservation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"variant-c-distributed-processing-with-an-outbox\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variant C:\nDistributed processing with an outbox<\/h3>\n<p>If job creation, credit reservation and recording the queue event are\ncombined in one local database transaction, the handover to the worker\ncan be made more reliable. The outbox dispatch takes place later. The\nworker handles repeated message deliveries idempotently.<\/p>\n<p>If the AI worker fails, the system can rerun the job or refund\ncredits depending on the business rule. Whether a partially generated\nimage file is retained, deleted or charged for must be decided at the\nbusiness level: database technology alone does not answer this\nquestion.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"variant-d-multiple-independent-account-databases\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variant D:\nMultiple independent account databases<\/h3>\n<p>If the credit balances of different customers are distributed across\nseparate shards, operations affecting only <strong>one<\/strong> customer\ncan often be handled within one shard. If credits must instead be\ntransferred between customers on different shards, a new coordination\nproblem arises. The choice of shard key from M3-L3 is therefore not only\nabout speed, but also about transaction boundaries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Insight:<\/strong> A scalable architecture does not need\nmaximum distribution everywhere. It helps to keep the critical business\ndecision as local and unambiguous as possible and deliberately execute\ndownstream work asynchronously.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operation, security, costs and troubleshooting<\/h2>\n<p>With every additional instance and service, not only capacity but\nalso the number of possible failure states increases. For correct\ndistributed processing, operators must therefore consider several\naspects:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Observability:<\/strong> Operations need unique identifiers\nthat can be found again in the database, message and application.\nOtherwise, duplicate or lost processing is difficult to reconstruct.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Monitoring:<\/strong> Alongside general server utilisation,\nconflict-related aborts, deadlocks, retry rates, queue backlogs, old\noutbox entries and long-running transactions are important signals. Low\nCPU utilisation does not rule out a serious locking bottleneck.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security boundaries:<\/strong> Servers may only access the\ndata and messages needed for their task. A lack of tenant isolation can\nexpose data even if transactions are technically correct. Idempotency\nkeys must not allow users to retrieve other users’ job results; they\nmust be bound to authorised identities and operations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backups and recovery:<\/strong> A transaction does not protect\nagainst every kind of data loss. If incorrect changes have been stored\ncorrectly, they remain correctly stored – and can be replicated.\nIndependent backups and tested recovery remain necessary. Details follow\nin M3-L6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Costs:<\/strong> Strict isolation, coordinated multi-node\ntransactions, additional tables, queue infrastructure and continuous\nmonitoring consume computing time, storage, network traffic and\nmaintenance. Troubleshooting also becomes more demanding. Costs arise\nnot only from rental prices but also from development and operational\nhours.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational decision:<\/strong> If the entire application\nworks reliably with one database and a few clearly defined transactions,\nthis is often the more economical architecture compared with a\ndistributed transaction apparatus. A complex mechanism should only be\nused when concrete business or operational requirements justify it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guidance for choosing the right mechanism<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Usually suitable starting solution<\/th>\n<th>Additional caution<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Change two fields of a local database together<\/td>\n<td>Ordinary ACID transaction<\/td>\n<td>Define business constraints correctly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concurrent reservation of the same seat<\/td>\n<td>Atomic decision, suitable isolation or lock<\/td>\n<td>Handle conflicts and retries correctly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same web job sent twice<\/td>\n<td>Unique operation identifier and idempotent processing<\/td>\n<td>Store result, identifier and permissions consistently<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Database change should later trigger a message<\/td>\n<td>Transactional outbox with idempotent recipient<\/td>\n<td>Dispatch can occur repeatedly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two independent databases must decide atomically<\/td>\n<td>Appropriate coordinated distributed transaction, if supported<\/td>\n<td>Recovery, locks and coordinator failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-running job across several external services<\/td>\n<td>Saga with documented states and compensation<\/td>\n<td>Not all real-world actions can be undone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public search index lags behind an image change<\/td>\n<td>Eventual consistency often acceptable<\/td>\n<td>Keep the delay visible and limited<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment or booking decision<\/td>\n<td>Authoritative, correctly coordinated data basis<\/td>\n<td>Permit neither duplicate effects nor conflicting confirmations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table is deliberately not a universal product recommendation. It\nfirst separates the <strong>correctness problem<\/strong> from the\nquestion of whether to solve it with PostgreSQL, SQLite, a managed\nservice or a distributed database.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M3-L6<\/h2>\n<p>Operating databases across several servers is not just a problem of\nspeed and data distribution. The more difficult question is often:\n<strong>What must under no circumstances happen during concurrent\nchanges and partial failures?<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>ACID transactions<\/strong> group related local changes;\nbusiness rules must be described correctly.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Isolation and locks<\/strong> coordinate concurrent access,\nbut can cause conflicts, waiting and deadlocks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Retries<\/strong> are important, but must not trigger the\nsame business action multiple times in an uncontrolled way.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Idempotency<\/strong> and unique operation identifiers\nprotect against unwanted duplicate effects.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A <strong>transactional outbox<\/strong> couples a data change and a\nrecorded pending message in one local transaction; recipients must\nhandle duplicates.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Two-Phase Commit<\/strong> coordinates atomic decisions\nacross systems but adds considerable operational requirements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sagas<\/strong> coordinate multi-step business processes\nusing local transactions and possible compensating actions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Eventual consistency<\/strong> may be useful for less\ncritical data views, but does not replace strict decisions where\nduplicate confirmations are impermissible.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>M3-L6 deals next with <strong>data durability<\/strong>: independent\nbackups, snapshots, point-in-time recovery and the question of how much\ndata loss and downtime a system can tolerate in the first place.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Terms and abbreviations at a glance<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Full term / meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ACID<\/td>\n<td>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability – transaction\nguarantees<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commit / Rollback<\/td>\n<td>Commit a transaction definitively / undo uncommitted changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MVCC<\/td>\n<td>Multi-Version Concurrency Control<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lock / Deadlock<\/td>\n<td>Lock / mutual blocking of transactions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serializable<\/td>\n<td>Isolation guarantee: effect as if some serial execution had\noccurred<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retry<\/td>\n<td>Deliberate repetition of a potentially failed operation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Idempotency<\/td>\n<td>Repeated identical request without repeated business-level\neffect<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HTTP<\/td>\n<td>Hypertext Transfer Protocol – web communication protocol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outbox / Inbox<\/td>\n<td>Stored outgoing / incoming messages or processing identifiers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2PC<\/td>\n<td>Two-Phase Commit – two-phase confirmation of a distributed\ntransaction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saga<\/td>\n<td>Sequence of local transactions with documented compensation\nsteps<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eventual consistency<\/td>\n<td>Eventual convergence of copies under suitable conditions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shard<\/td>\n<td>Independent part of a partitioned database<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worker<\/td>\n<td>Background process that processes messages or jobs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reviewed on 03.11.2026.<\/strong> Relevant primary\ndocumentation and standards:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Transaction Isolation<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— standard isolation levels, anomalies, Serializable and\nPostgreSQL-specific characteristics.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Explicit Locking<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— locks, deadlocks and conflict avoidance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Serialization Failure Handling<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— retrying entire transactions after serialisation failures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Two-Phase Transactions<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/two-phase.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/two-phase.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— support for prepared transactions with an external coordinator.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">RFC 9110, <em>HTTP Semantics<\/em>, section 9.2.2: <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.2.2\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.2.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— idempotency of HTTP methods and retry semantics.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Microsoft Learn, <em>Compensating Transaction pattern<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/compensating-transaction\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/compensating-transaction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— limitations and significance of compensating transactions and their\nrelation to sagas.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Microsoft Learn, <em>Transactional Outbox pattern<\/em> (Azure Cosmos\nDB): <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/databases/guide/transactional-out-box-cosmos\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/databases/guide/transactional-out-box-cosmos<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— durably recorded pending events and asynchronous publication.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Microsoft Learn, <em>Implement the Transactional Outbox pattern with\nDataverse<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/architecture/reference-architectures/transactional-outbox-pattern\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/architecture/reference-architectures/transactional-outbox-pattern<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— duplicate message delivery and idempotent recipients.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> The lesson compares architectural principles.\nWhich isolation level, delivery guarantee or configuration a particular\nproduct actually provides must be verified separately for its version\nand operating mode. No independent load tests or production payments,\nbookings or clusters are carried out here.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M3-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M3-L6 – Data durability: Backups, recovery and failure preparedness<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor’s / transition: Database systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: Data must survive a failure<\/h2>\n<p>A database is not secure simply because it runs on a reliable server.\nA computer can fail, a storage device can be damaged, human error can\ndelete thousands of records, a software fault can overwrite content\nincorrectly, or an attacker can encrypt data and attack backups. Even a\nperfectly functioning database server can permanently store a deletion\nthat is <em>technically valid but wrong from a business\nperspective<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data durability<\/strong> initially means that committed\nchanges survive certain technical failures. In practice, this property\nis not sufficient: an operator must also be prepared to recover the data\nafter accidental or malicious changes. That is precisely the purpose of\nindependent <strong>backups<\/strong> and planned <strong>recovery\nprocedures<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A small media catalogue, a SaaS service (Software as a Service,\nsoftware delivered over the internet) and a paid AI job processing\nservice require different degrees of protection. The greater the damage\ncaused by interruption or data loss, the more extensive the backup\narrangements, monitoring, testing and organisational responsibilities\nusually become.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Running application\n        │\n        ▼\nProduction database ───────► Independent backup\n        │                          │\n        │ Technical failure        │ Retained, verified copy\n        ▼                          ▼\nData missing / incorrect ──► Recovery\n                                   │\n                                   ▼\n                             Verified data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This lesson does not explain how to administer a particular server\nusing commands. Instead, it discusses the available protection\nmechanisms, the failures they address and the new responsibilities they\nintroduce.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four things that look similar but serve different\npurposes<\/h2>\n<p>In everyday use, <strong>replication<\/strong>,\n<strong>backups<\/strong>, <strong>snapshots<\/strong> and\n<strong>archiving<\/strong> are often confused. However, they answer\ndifferent questions.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method<\/th>\n<th>Main purpose<\/th>\n<th>Typical strength<\/th>\n<th>Typical limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Replication<\/td>\n<td>Keep data on other running nodes up to date<\/td>\n<td>Faster takeover in certain server failures; potentially additional\nreaders<\/td>\n<td>Incorrect changes and deletions can also be replicated<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backup<\/td>\n<td>Retain older, preferably independent and recoverable data\nstates<\/td>\n<td>Recovery after deletion, corruption or failure<\/td>\n<td>Recovery takes time; the backup may be incomplete<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snapshot<\/td>\n<td>Capture the state of a storage system or service at a particular\npoint in time<\/td>\n<td>Often very quick to create<\/td>\n<td>Without appropriate consistency and independent retention, not a\nreliable substitute for a backup<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Archiving<\/td>\n<td>Retain data or backups over the long term<\/td>\n<td>Historical traceability and long-term preservation<\/td>\n<td>An archive is not necessarily quickly recoverable or recoverable as\na running database<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For example, if the customer table is accidentally deleted at 14:00,\na second database server maintained through synchronous replication can\nreceive the same deletion. It remains technically available, but\ncontains the same error. An independent backup from 13:00 could save the\ndata. However, changes between 13:00 and 14:00 would be missing if no\nsupplementary change logs had been retained.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> A purely public, immutable\ndemonstration project whose data can be regenerated entirely from\nversion control or other original sources might not require extensive\ndatabase backups. Nevertheless, application recovery and the original\nsources must be protected.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How a database makes its changes durable in the first\nplace<\/h2>\n<p>In M3-L1 we encountered <strong>Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)<\/strong>:\nin many systems, database changes are first recorded in a log before the\naffected data pages are updated durably at a later point. This helps in\nthe event of a sudden crash. After restarting, the server can determine\nwhich changes are considered complete and restore a consistent state\nusing the log.<\/p>\n<p>However, WAL alone is <strong>not an external backup<\/strong>. If the\ndata files and logs are lost together, it offers no benefit. Nor does\nthe log automatically protect against a deletion correctly executed by\nthe database system. The distinction is this: <em>crash recovery<\/em>\nrestores internal consistency after a particular system failure;\n<em>backup recovery<\/em> reconstructs data from separately retained\nbackups.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                 Transaction\n                      │\n                      ▼\n                Change log (WAL)\n                      │\n          ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n          ▼                       ▼\n        Commit                Data files\n                                  │\n                             Later write\n\nExternal backup: separate copy + archived WAL segments if required<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Precisely how a commit (final confirmation of a transaction) is\nprotected depends on the database system, its settings and the storage\nhardware. The operating system, file system, storage controller and\nbehaviour during a power failure remain relevant parts of the protection\nchain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simple backups: A complete copy or logical export<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>full backup<\/strong> contains a complete, recoverable\nstarting state. In PostgreSQL such a backup can take different\nforms.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>logical backup<\/strong> exports database objects and data\nin a form from which they can be reconstructed. PostgreSQL provides\n<code>pg_dump<\/code> for this purpose; <code>pg_dumpall<\/code>\nadditionally covers multiple databases and global objects such as roles.\nSuch an export is suitable, among other things, for smaller data sets,\nmigrations and recovery of selected database objects. However, it is not\nthe same as a physical backup of an entire database cluster.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>physical backup<\/strong> retains the database files in a\nform usable by the system. In PostgreSQL, <code>pg_basebackup<\/code> is\na tool intended for creating a consistent base backup. An advantage can\nbe faster recovery of large data sets; version compatibility,\ncompleteness of files and matching change logs are particularly\nimportant in return.<\/p>\n<p>The official PostgreSQL documentation distinguishes three basic\nstrategies: SQL dump, file system backup and continuous archiving. The\nmethods are not arbitrarily interchangeable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                  PostgreSQL\n                      │\n          ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n          ▼                       ▼\n      Logical export        Physical backup\n      Tables + schema        Database files\n          │                       │\n          ▼                       ▼\n        Reimport             Cluster restore<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> An export that nobody can import\nsuccessfully is merely a file, not a backup with proven functionality.\nWith large databases, both export and import can take a long time. The\nbackup should also account for the required software version, credential\nmanagement and configuration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Snapshots: Quick to create, but useful only under certain\nconditions<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>snapshot<\/strong> captures a state, for example of a\nstorage device or a cloud storage volume. Many storage systems can\ncreate such states quickly and subsequently manage changed blocks\nseparately. This makes snapshots attractive for short rollbacks,\nmaintenance or extra layers of protection.<\/p>\n<p>The critical concept is <strong>consistency<\/strong>. A database\noften writes several interrelated files and logs. If only some of them\nare captured at an unsuitable moment, the result may not be a usable\ndatabase state. Depending on the database system, a\n<em>crash-consistent<\/em> snapshot may be usable with the system’s own\nrecovery log; an <em>application-consistent<\/em> backup additionally\ntakes account of the specific requirements of the database. Whether a\nsnapshot is usable must be verified for the particular platform.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Database running ──► Coordinated snapshot\n                           │\n                           ▼\n                       Captured state\n                           │\n                           ▼\n                     Separate retention\n                           │\n                           ▼\n                       Test recovery<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A snapshot stored exclusively on the same storage system as the\nproduction database is often insufficiently protected against the\ncomplete loss of that system. Cloud snapshots may likewise share access\npermissions, account risks or failure domains with the original.\n<strong>A separately retained backup whose recoverability can be tested\nis more important than the name of the method.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Full, incremental and differential backups<\/h2>\n<p>Not every backup needs to transfer all the data again. This leads to\nthree fundamental strategies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Full backup:<\/strong> At a particular time, all the required\ndata is backed up. Recovery is comparatively straightforward, but backup\nduration and storage requirements can increase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Incremental backup:<\/strong> After an initial backup, only\ncertain changes since an earlier backup state are retained. This can\nsave space but creates dependencies: recovery requires the appropriate\nbase and the necessary sequence of changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Differential backup:<\/strong> Usually the changes since a\nparticular full backup are collected. Recovery then requires, for\nexample, the full backup and a subsequent differential backup. Not every\nproduct uses these terms or methods in the same way.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Full backup on Monday\n     │\n     ├── Tuesday: changes A\n     ├── Wednesday: changes B\n     └── Thursday: changes C\n\nIncremental: Full + A + B + C (depending on the method)\nDifferential: Full + combined changes A/B/C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The PostgreSQL 18 project documentation also describes\n<strong>incremental physical base backups<\/strong>. They require\nsuitable previous backups and reconstruction using appropriate tools;\nthe project explicitly points out that the required backup dependencies\nare not automatically managed on behalf of the operator. For small\ndatabases, regular full backups are therefore often simpler and more\neconomical. Incremental strategies can help with large data sets that\nchange relatively little.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational risk:<\/strong> A missing intermediate backup or a\ndamaged required file can render an entire recovery chain unusable. More\ncomplex backup methods require careful retention rules and checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Point-in-time recovery: Returning to a specific point in\ntime<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR)<\/strong> means recovery to a\nselected earlier point in time. It is particularly helpful when one\nknows <em>when<\/em> an error began but does not simply want to reset all\nthe data to the last daily backup.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a small online service: at 14:07 a faulty update deletes a\ntable. The last full backup dates from 02:00. Without further backups,\nthe service would have to return to its state at 02:00. Changes from\ntwelve hours and seven minutes would be lost. With a suitable physical\nbase backup and <strong>continuously archived WAL files<\/strong>,\nPostgreSQL can replay changes up to the moment immediately before the\nerror.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>02:00       10:00       14:06       14:07       15:00\n  │            │           │            │           │\n  ▼            ▼           ▼            ▼           ▼\nBase backup ───────── WAL change sequence ──────►\n                           ▲            ▲\n                           │            └─ Incorrect deletion\n                           └─ Possible recovery target\n\nRecovery = suitable base + complete WAL chain\n           + deliberately selected stopping point<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The mechanism has several prerequisites. The base backup must match\nthe WAL logs. The required segments must all be present and readable,\nwithout gaps. The correct target time must also be identifiable.\nPostgreSQL can stop during recovery at a specified timestamp or named\nrecovery point.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important limitation:<\/strong> An ordinary\n<code>pg_dump<\/code> cannot simply be combined with later WAL segments\nto form a PITR chain. The base used for this purpose is an appropriate\nphysical backup. And a stopping point that is too late would replay the\nincorrect deletion.<\/p>\n<p>PITR also does not mean that the work involved is trivial. A large\ndata set may first have to be restored in full and then many changes\nreplayed. This can significantly increase recovery time. That is\nprecisely why backup and recovery should be planned together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>RPO and RTO: Two distinct requirements<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_023_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Difference between replication and independent backups, with an example timeline from the last recovery point through a failure to service restoration; RPO and RTO.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Difference between replication and\nindependent backups, with an example timeline from the last recovery\npoint through a failure to service restoration; RPO and\nRTO.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Example with the last recoverable state at 10:45, a\nfailure at 11:00 and service restoration at 12:00. The illustrated\nintervals of 15 and 60 minutes are assumed actual intervals and\n<strong>not<\/strong> guaranteed RPO/RTO objectives. Replication alone\ndoes not reliably protect against logically incorrect or deliberately\ndeleted data.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Computer science uses two metrics to specify the acceptable\nconsequences of a failure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RPO – Recovery Point Objective:<\/strong> the target for how\nfar back a recovered data state may be relative to the moment of\nfailure. Intuitively, it is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in\ntime. An RPO of one hour, for example, means that the operator <em>aims\nto tolerate<\/em> the loss of no more than approximately one hour of\nchanges in a specified failure scenario. This is a requirement, not an\nautomatic guarantee.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RTO – Recovery Time Objective:<\/strong> the target for the\ntime required for a system to return to a usable state after a failure.\nAn RTO of four hours means that recovery is intended to take place\nwithin that time frame. This too must be demonstrated technically and\norganisationally.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>RPO – How OLD may the recovered data be at most?\n\nLast reliably recoverable change               Failure\n              │                                    │\n              └──────────── Data gap ──────────────┘\n\nRTO – How LONG may recovery take?\n\n             Failure                    Service usable again\n                │                               │\n                └──────── Interruption ─────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hypothetical service<\/th>\n<th>Example RPO target<\/th>\n<th>Example RTO target<\/th>\n<th>Rationale<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private media catalogue that rarely changes<\/td>\n<td>24 hours<\/td>\n<td>1–2 days<\/td>\n<td>Changes can be reconstructed if necessary<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small online administration tool<\/td>\n<td>1 hour<\/td>\n<td>4 hours<\/td>\n<td>Users expect limited data loss and a manageable interruption<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Booking or credit balance service<\/td>\n<td>Very low, possibly close to zero<\/td>\n<td>Very low<\/td>\n<td>Confirmed bookings may be financially binding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These values are <strong>freely chosen teaching examples<\/strong> and\nexplicitly not industry standards. Even an RPO close to zero does not\nautomatically require sharding or very many servers, but an architecture\ntailored to specific failures. An RTO of zero is practically\nunattainable for most failure models.<\/p>\n<p>A short interruption and low data loss are <strong>two separate\nobjectives<\/strong>. Switching to a replica can restore operations\nquickly and still lose some of the most recent committed changes.\nConversely, a full backup can provide all the required data but take\nmany hours to restore.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Offsite backups, 3-2-1 and protection against shared\nfailures<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>offsite copy<\/strong> is a backup retained outside the\nprimary operating location or failure domain. The central idea is to\nprevent all copies from being lost through the same cause. If the\nproduction data and backup reside on the same server and storage device,\na single fault can destroy both.<\/p>\n<p>A familiar mnemonic is <strong>3-2-1<\/strong>: three copies of\nimportant data (including production data), on two different storage\nmedia or systems, one of which is geographically or technically\nseparate. For modern online services, it is more important than a rigid\nnumber to have <strong>independent failure, access and deletion\npaths<\/strong>. A cloud backup under the same administrative identity\ncan also be at risk if access is compromised.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Production database\n       │\n       ├── Local backup (quick recovery)\n       │\n       └── Separate backup (offsite)\n                │\n                ├── Restricted write/delete permissions\n                ├── Immutable retention period if needed\n                └── Verified recoverability<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An <strong>immutable backup<\/strong> means that a retained backup\nstate cannot readily be overwritten or deleted during a specified\nprotection period. An <strong>offline backup<\/strong> refers to a copy\ntemporarily or permanently disconnected from the running system. Both\nproperties can help protect against attacks, but they are not\nidentical.<\/p>\n<p>The US agencies FBI and NIST emphasise separate or immutable backups\nand practical recovery tests in their security guidance. Such\nrecommendations serve as orientation; the specific strategy needed\ndepends on the data and risks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A backup must account for more than a database file<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose an online service’s database has been recovered. The website\nstill does not start because configuration is missing, a key is\nunavailable, uploaded files were not backed up or the application\nversion is incompatible with the data. In that case, <em>only part<\/em>\nof the service has been recovered.<\/p>\n<p>Complete recovery planning may also require:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Database schema, database roles, permissions and required\nextensions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Application version, configuration, infrastructure description and\ndocumentation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Uploaded files, images, documents or external object storage\ndata.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Secrets and encryption keys – securely separated and with controlled\naccess.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Domain/network configuration, external dependencies, identity and\npayment services.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A comprehensible sequence for restoring and testing the\napplication.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Not every component must be retained using the same backup method.\nProgram code, for instance, can be recovered from version control\nprovided that access and versions are also protected. Irreplaceable\ncustomer data normally needs stronger protection than static application\nfiles.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Particular risk: encryption keys.<\/strong> If data is stored\nencrypted but the only decryption key is lost, it may no longer be\npossible to read even a technically intact backup. The key, in turn,\nmust not be left completely unprotected right beside the backup.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security and data protection in backups<\/h2>\n<p>A backup often contains the same sensitive content as the production\nsystem: customer data, internal documents and possibly access\ninformation. Every additional copy creates another opportunity for\naccess and another retention obligation.<\/p>\n<p>Useful protection principles include:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Access minimisation:<\/strong> Only explicitly authorised\npersons and services may read, restore or delete backups. This protects\nin particular against accidental and malicious deletion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Encryption:<\/strong> Transmission and storage can be\nencrypted. Key management and the recoverability of keys are part of the\noverall solution.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Retention period:<\/strong> Do not keep copies indefinitely\nwithout a purpose. Longer histories help with late-discovered corruption\nbut can cause data protection and storage problems.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Separation:<\/strong> Where possible, protect particularly\nvaluable backups separately from the same administrative permissions,\nthe running server and shared causes of failure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Traceability:<\/strong> Who created, deleted, verified or\nrestored which backup, and when? Logs themselves require protection.<\/p>\n<p>Personal data may raise questions of deletion and retention under\ndata protection law. A backup is not a separate collection exempt from\nlegal obligations; specific requirements must be assessed according to\ncountry, legal basis, contract and technical design. This chapter is not\nlegal advice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Restore testing: The central practical test<\/h2>\n<p>A file called <code>backup-2026-11-03.zip<\/code> initially proves\nonly that a file exists. It does not establish whether it is complete,\nreadable, correct and recoverable on another system. Even a successful\nchecksum verifies only a limited subset of the required properties.<\/p>\n<p>A genuine <strong>restore test<\/strong> (<em>recovery test<\/em>)\nrestores a backup into a suitable test environment. Data and application\nare then checked: Are the tables present? Are the expected records and\nrelationships correct? Do login, essential queries and business\nprocesses work? How long did the entire procedure take?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Backup\n      │\n      ▼\nIntegrity check (files/manifest)\n      │\n      ▼\nRecovery in test environment\n      │\n      ▼\nStart database + application\n      │\n      ▼\nBusiness-level test + time measurement\n      │\n      ▼\nDocumented evidence<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>PostgreSQL provides <code>pg_verifybackup<\/code>, a tool that\nverifies suitable physical base backups against a backup manifest.\nHowever, the project documentation explicitly warns that such checks\n<strong>cannot replace a complete restore test<\/strong>. Errors may\nexist that only become visible during startup or business-level\nqueries.<\/p>\n<p>A useful test separately assesses <strong>data integrity<\/strong> (is\nthe backup technically correct?), <strong>business-level\nplausibility<\/strong> (is it the required data state?) and\n<strong>operability<\/strong> (can users actually use the service\nagain?).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A recovery plan needs decisions, not just technology<\/h2>\n<p>During a failure, not every step is immediately clear. Should the old\nserver be repaired or a new system built? Which backup state should be\nused? Must particular payments or changes be reconciled manually\nafterwards? How can users be prevented from creating further\ncontradictory data during a partial recovery?<\/p>\n<p>A realistic procedure might look like this:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Detect and isolate the failure:<\/strong> A hardware failure\nonly, a lost database file, an incorrect change or an attack?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Prevent further damage:<\/strong> Restrict problematic write\naccess where possible without additional data loss; protect unchanged\nbackups.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Select the recovery target:<\/strong> Last intact state, a\ndefined point in time or a still-intact replica?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Prepare the new environment:<\/strong> Provide suitable\nsoftware version, configuration, keys and storage capacity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Restore data:<\/strong> From a complete backup and any\nnecessary change logs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Verify technically and at business level:<\/strong> Is the\ndata consistent, are expected records present and are application\nfunctions usable?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Restore service in a controlled way:<\/strong> Inform users,\nreconcile pending operations and document events afterwards.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This sequence is guidance, not a universal emergency manual. If an\nattack is suspected, a compromised system must not automatically be\ntreated as a trusted starting point. Financially binding operations may\nrequire further coordination and documented decisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three continuous practical cases<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"case-a-local-media-catalogue-with-sqlite\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case A: Local media\ncatalogue with SQLite<\/h3>\n<p>A locally developed media catalogue stores file paths, categories,\nratings and notes in SQLite. The database resides in a file on a single\ncomputer. For a manageable application, this is often a very good,\nsimple solution.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backup problem:<\/strong> If the SSD fails, personally created\ncategories and notes are lost. Copying the file every night can be\nproblematic while the database is being accessed, depending on the\njournal mode and procedure. The official SQLite documentation provides\nthe <strong>Online Backup API<\/strong> (an application programming\ninterface for consistent online copies) for backups during operation and\nalternatively <code>VACUUM INTO<\/code>. Both can create a consistent\ndatabase copy without requiring an installation project here.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>SQLite database\n       │\n       ▼\nConsistent online backup\n       │\n       ├── Local copy\n       └── Separate archive\n                │\n                ▼\n          Open as a test<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>It is also necessary to consider whether the catalogued media files\nthemselves require backups or whether they can be recovered from an\nexisting archive. <strong>A backed-up SQLite catalogue file does not\nautomatically protect the catalogued media.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"case-b-small-online-service-with-postgresql\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case B: Small\nonline service with PostgreSQL<\/h3>\n<p>A service receives customer settings, jobs and status information. A\nsmall rented server and a single PostgreSQL database may be sufficient\nfor operation.<\/p>\n<p>An obvious backup strategy consists of regular database exports or\nsuitable physical backups, a separate copy and a documented restore. If\na few hours of data loss are unacceptable, a combination of physical\nbase backups and continuous WAL archiving may become useful. The price\nis storage, monitoring, more complex recovery and recurring tests.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Web application → PostgreSQL\n                     │\n              Base backup + WAL archive\n                     │\n              Separate backup destination\n                     │\n                 Controlled restore<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An additional standby can mitigate certain failures more quickly but\nreplaces neither this backup nor the correction of business-level\nerrors. For a service that generates only a few changes every day, a\nsimpler backup may be more economical.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"case-c-ai-job-processing-service-with-database-and-object-storage\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case\nC: AI job processing service with database and object storage<\/h3>\n<p>An online AI service manages user credit balances, image/video\nuploads, processing jobs and finished results. Important information is\nnot exclusively stored in a database. Job data might reside in\nPostgreSQL, input and output files in separate object storage, and\nactual processing might run on one or more GPU servers (Graphics\nProcessing Units, graphics/parallel processors).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>           User\n             │\n             ▼\n      Job management\n        │          │\n        ▼          ▼\n   PostgreSQL   Object storage\n        │          │\n        └────┬─────┘\n             ▼\n          AI worker\n             │\n             ▼\n       Result + status\n\nBackups: DB state + files + associations<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If only the database is restored from the previous night but object\nstorage files are retained in their current state, records may refer to\nnon-existent files or other files may no longer have associated\nmetadata. A sound recovery strategy must account for <strong>consistent\nrelationships across multiple stores<\/strong>. This may require a shared\nbackup strategy, traceable object versions and verifiable job\nidentifiers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cost trap:<\/strong> Large image and video files, multiple\nbackup versions and transfers from remote storage can generate\nsubstantial recurring costs. Not every generated intermediate result\nmust be retained forever. Particularly for a small service, it is\nimportant first to establish which data is truly indispensable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How to choose the right backup strategy<\/h2>\n<p>Five guiding questions are useful for a specific application:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Implication<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Which data is irreplaceable?<\/td>\n<td>The harder it is to reconstruct, the more important an independent\nbackup becomes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How much data loss is acceptable?<\/td>\n<td>This determines the RPO requirement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How long may the service be unavailable?<\/td>\n<td>This determines the RTO requirement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which failures must be addressed?<\/td>\n<td>Hardware failure, human error, deletion, attack and data centre\nfailure require partly different measures<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who operates and verifies the procedure?<\/td>\n<td>Backup without responsible operators, alerts and restore testing\nremains risky<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For a private project, a consistent daily database export with a\nseparate copy may be sufficient. For a service with paying customers,\nmore frequent backups, longer histories or a verified recovery procedure\nmay be necessary. For particularly critical data, multiple independent\nmethods may be appropriate.<\/p>\n<p>However, one should <strong>not automatically choose the most\nexpensive architecture<\/strong>. More backups, more replicas and more\nautomation also increase the number of potentially misconfigured\ncomponents. A simple, tested backup is more valuable than a complex\nsystem that nobody can reliably recover.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operating costs and typical misconceptions<\/h2>\n<p>The cost of a backup architecture is not simply a matter of gigabytes\nof storage. It also includes computing and input/output load during\ncreation, network traffic, possible data retrieval fees, retention\nperiods, monitoring, key management and staff time for testing. With\nlarge databases, recovery duration can also substantially affect\noperational effort.<\/p>\n<p>Typical misconceptions include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“We have a replica, so we have a backup.”<\/strong> No; many\nerrors are also replicated.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“Snapshots are always complete backups.”<\/strong> No;\nconsistency, retention and independence must be verified.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“A daily export guarantees zero data loss.”<\/strong> No;\nchanges since the last protected state may be missing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“A successful backup means a successful restore.”<\/strong>\nNo; only an actual verified recovery attempt demonstrates much of its\npractical usability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“Encrypted automatically means secure.”<\/strong> No; key\nloss and compromised permissions remain risks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“Cloud data is always fully protected by the\nprovider.”<\/strong> No; contractual service limits and shared\noperational responsibility must be examined.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>“A backup concerns only the database.”<\/strong> No; files,\nconfiguration, secrets and external services can be just as\nimportant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The goal is <strong>appropriate, demonstrably functional\nrecoverability<\/strong>, not maximum complexity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M4-L1<\/h2>\n<p>Data durability consists of several interacting properties. The\ndatabase itself protects committed transactions against certain crashes.\nReplication increases availability under suitable conditions. Backups\nretain independent recovery states. Snapshots can help, but without\nconsistency checks and independent storage they do not provide complete\nprotection. Point-in-time recovery can reconstruct changes up to a point\nbefore an error if the base backup and log chain are complete and\ncompatible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RPO<\/strong> answers the question of acceptable data loss,\nwhereas <strong>RTO<\/strong> concerns acceptable recovery time. Both are\nobjectives that must be verified through genuine recovery tests. The\nmost important practical insight is this: <strong>A backup is only truly\nuseful if the required data can be recovered within an appropriate\ntime.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This concludes the particularly detailed section <strong>C –\nDistributing databases across multiple servers<\/strong>. In the next\nsection, <strong>M4-L1 – Distributed processing<\/strong>, we move beyond\ndata storage alone and examine how message brokers, queues and workers\ncoordinate complex tasks across multiple computers. Especially for\nautomated media and AI workflows, this forms the bridge from the\ndatabase to actual service operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The following official and specialist sources were reviewed on\n<strong>03.11.2026<\/strong>; details may change with software versions.\nThe teaching examples are independent illustrations, not production\nconfiguration instructions.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Backup and Restore<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/backup.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/backup.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— SQL dumps, file system backups, continuous archiving.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>Continuous Archiving and Point-in-Time\nRecovery<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— WAL base, PITR, physical/incremental backups, dependencies and\nlimitations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>pg_verifybackup<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgverifybackup.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgverifybackup.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— verification of backup manifests; explicit limitations compared with\nactual restore tests.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">PostgreSQL 18, <em>pg_dumpall<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pg-dumpall.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pg-dumpall.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— global database objects, roles and multiple databases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">SQLite, <em>Online Backup API<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— consistent online backups and the alternative\n<code>VACUUM INTO<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">NIST, <em>Recovery Point Objective<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/recovery_point_objective\">https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/recovery_point_objective<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— concept and scope of the data recovery objective.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">NIST, <em>Recovery Time Objective<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/Recovery_Time_Objective\">https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/Recovery_Time_Objective<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— concept and scope of recovery time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">FBI, <em>Ten Actions to Improve Cyber Resiliency<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.fbi.gov/investigate/cyber/ten-actions-to-improve-cyber-resiliency\">https://www.fbi.gov/investigate/cyber/ten-actions-to-improve-cyber-resiliency<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n— separate/immutable backups and restore tests.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Technical limitations:<\/strong> Example RPO/RTO values are\nhypothetical. PostgreSQL and SQLite procedures depend on versions and\nconfiguration. No production recoveries, cost measurements or\ncase-specific legal assessments were carried out.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
